WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.065 --> 00:00:08.640
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, welcome everybody&nbsp;
to this public session of the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.240
of the NHS Board.&nbsp; And we should just note&nbsp;
that in line with government guidance we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.200
having – we’re not having a meeting all together&nbsp;
with members of the public for this session.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:27.440
So, apologies for absence.&nbsp; Susan Kilsby and&nbsp;
Munir Pirmohamed have sent in their apologies;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.640
and Patrick Carter, his connection&nbsp;
has been lost over the last hour, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.720
he may or may not join later on.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Most important, this is Amanda’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.800
public Board meeting as Chief Executive.&nbsp; And on&nbsp;
behalf of everybody, I know all the Members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:51.440
Board, Amanda, many congratulations, well done.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And we all look forward to working with you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.000
Any declarations of interest&nbsp;
that are not already recorded?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.400
No.&nbsp; And can I approve the minutes of&nbsp;
the last meeting?&nbsp; Good.&nbsp; Thank you very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:11.840
much.&nbsp; And so the first item, it’s the Chief&nbsp;
Executive’s update.&nbsp; Over to you, Amanda.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:23.200
Deathly silence.&nbsp; Are you there, Amanda?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Hello, apologies.&nbsp; I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.920
muted my speakers inadvertently.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.360
fire away.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:35.360
Well, thank you very much indeed, David.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
I’m absolutely delighted to be addressing my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.920
first Board meeting as Chief Executive of NHS&nbsp;
England.&nbsp; And I would just like to start by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.440
thanking you and indeed the rest of the Board for&nbsp;
your support and counsel in these first weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.760
It’s been a summer, I think, of unprecedented&nbsp;
pressure, but also of real achievement.&nbsp; Among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.520
the other advances, we’ve started the world’s&nbsp;
largest trial of the revolutionary new NHS-Galleri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.160
blood test that can detect more than 50 types&nbsp;
of cancer before symptoms appear.&nbsp; We’ve agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.280
a first-in-Europe access deal for a revolutionary&nbsp;
new treatment for certain types of lung cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.800
We’ve announced the rollout of heart checks&nbsp;
in community pharmacies, cholesterol‑busting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.640
jabs and genetic testing for a rare form of&nbsp;
diabetes.&nbsp; And of course, we’ve also launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.600
the next phase of the COVID vaccination programme&nbsp;
with our teams now in schools and delivering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.480
booster doses for those who are eligible.&nbsp;
In fact, today we’ve had confirmation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:34.080
the NHS COVID vaccination programme has prevented&nbsp;
more than 127,000 deaths and helped avoid more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:42.000
than 261,000 hospital admissions.&nbsp; I’m sure the&nbsp;
Board would want to join me in thanking everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.480
who works in the NHS, who’s been involved&nbsp;
in delivering these improvements, but also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.480
of course, thanking everyone dealing with the very&nbsp;
tough day-to-day pressures we are still facing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.320
Those teams, despite the pressure, have been able&nbsp;
to make excellent progress on restoring services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.600
which were inevitably impacted by COVID in the&nbsp;
first four months of the year.&nbsp; And I know we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.520
going to talk more about this, but it’s just worth&nbsp;
saying, you know, we did see 5&nbsp;million people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.480
start elective treatment, achieving 90%&nbsp;
of pre-pandemic levels.&nbsp; In the first four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:23.200
months of the year, we also did 7.5 million&nbsp;
key diagnostic tests, which is back over 95%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:31.840
of pre-pandemic levels, and we had 870,000 urgent&nbsp;
cancer checks, more than the same period in 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:39.920
GPs and their teams have delivered over&nbsp;
124 million routine appointments between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.520
April and August, more than the same&nbsp;
period in 2019.&nbsp; And at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.000
let’s not forget they have also delivered the&nbsp;
majority of our vaccination appointments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:57.360
So, I think this progress does provide a really&nbsp;
strong foundation for continued action in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.760
of those areas.&nbsp; And indeed, the welcome&nbsp;
clarity that we now have on funding for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.160
the second half of the financial year means&nbsp;
that over the last couple of weeks, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.000
been speaking to local leaders to update them on&nbsp;
plans and priorities for the next six months.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.680
Our planning guidance does set this out in full,&nbsp;
but I did want to focus, if I may, on three areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:27.120
which I know are front of mind for our patients.&nbsp;
So, on elective care, our focus will be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.520
continuing to achieve the maximum possible level&nbsp;
of activity, but prioritising particularly those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.320
who, of course, are the most urgent patients,&nbsp;
as well as those who have waited the longest.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.120
£1.5 billion is being made available to help local&nbsp;
teams increase their capacity and invest in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.040
proven measures to achieve these goals&nbsp;
like surgical hubs for high volume work.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:56.000
We also now have clarity on updated infection&nbsp;
prevention and control guidance from government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.080
and we’re going to be working with providers&nbsp;
to help them apply those new guidelines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:08.080
Secondly, on cancer services our focus will be on&nbsp;
meeting the faster diagnosis standard that’s 28&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.600
days from GP referral to diagnosis or indeed, of&nbsp;
course, ruling out cancer; and, as well, improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:20.320
performance on the 62-day treatment standard.&nbsp;
On primary care, we’re going to be making further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.840
targeted investment available to ensure GP&nbsp;
practices are able to offer timely appointment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.640
options for those who need them.&nbsp; And that,&nbsp;
of course, will lead to include respecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.640
patient preferences for face-to-face care, but&nbsp;
also recognising that for many, digital access,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:42.800
telephone access, is more convenient.&nbsp; We&nbsp;
also need to continue to increase the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.880
of community pharmacies, so patients can&nbsp;
access consultations and treatments for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.000
minor illnesses and get support with their&nbsp;
new prescriptions after being in hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:58.160
Look, it’s no secret that the direct and indirect&nbsp;
effects of the pandemic continue to place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.320
a huge strain on both our physical capacity&nbsp;
and our staff.&nbsp; The conversations we’ve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:11.120
with local leaders over the past few weeks&nbsp;
have really highlighted that it’s not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.920
volume of people who now need urgent care that’s&nbsp;
putting some of the pressure on the system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.640
but it really is about acuity as well.&nbsp; Again,&nbsp;
I know we’re going to talk more about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:27.360
but just worth giving a few headlines.
The numbers, I think, are quite revealing here.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:35.280
So, we currently still have over 5,000 inpatients&nbsp;
with COVID in our hospitals.&nbsp; That is up from less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:43.040
than 800 back in May and around 600 this time last&nbsp;
year.&nbsp; In the first five months of this financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.440
year, teams have dealt with over 10 million A&amp;E&nbsp;
attendances and 3.9 million ambulance incidents.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:57.200
But within those numbers, that includes&nbsp;
100,000 more attendances at type one A&amp;Es,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:03.840
so the most unwell, sickest patients, than the&nbsp;
same period in 2019; and a higher proportion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:10.320
of category one and two ambulance incidents.&nbsp;
So, it has been an incredibly tough few months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.680
and it’s worth remembering&nbsp;
that it is only September.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.640
So even accounting for the vaccine&nbsp;
effect, without which things would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:24.560
much worse, and that’s clear if you look at the&nbsp;
PHE analysis I shared a moment ago, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.840
look, we can and we should reasonably assume&nbsp;
things will get even tougher over winter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.160
So, alongside that progress on elective&nbsp;
and cancer and investment in primary care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:42.240
the planning guidance also asks local teams to&nbsp;
prioritise continuing to deliver the flu and COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:48.000
vaccination programmes, reducing the number and&nbsp;
duration of ambulance handover delays, eliminating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.440
12-hour waits in A&amp;E, rolling out community crisis&nbsp;
response teams in every part of the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.880
and ensuring that patients can return home&nbsp;
as soon as it’s safe for them to do so.&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.920
It is going to be really important we deliver&nbsp;
on these priorities and do everything we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.480
to deliver the maximum possible benefit for&nbsp;
patients from the additional funding that they,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:12.720
of course, as taxpayers are providing.&nbsp;
I know people know this, but worth just saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.960
you know, it is it is worth&nbsp;
remembering we are one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:21.440
efficient health services in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;
But, of course, we do need to continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:28.640
optimise how we provide care, and this really&nbsp;
does include I think at its heart, making the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:36.320
possible use, if you like, of our most important&nbsp;
asset, and that’s our stuff.&nbsp; So that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:42.720
continuing to make it clear that it&nbsp;
is our diversity that is our strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.440
and that has always been the case&nbsp;
for the NHS; it remains so now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:54.720
It also means that we need to make absolutely&nbsp;
clear that the investment in health and wellbeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:02.000
needs to continue over the course of this year and&nbsp;
indeed beyond.&nbsp; And what that means in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:08.000
is making sure that we are supporting and&nbsp;
encouraging people to have their annual flu jabs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.840
and of course this year COVID boosters&nbsp;
too, because that’s something that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:16.880
can definitely do to protect themselves and their&nbsp;
colleagues.&nbsp; And whilst I know many organisations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:22.960
are really well advanced in delivering both&nbsp;
flu and COVID boosters, I would really want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.680
just say to anybody who’s listening, please&nbsp;
do take up that offer as soon as you can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:36.480
I’m sure that the Board and the public would also&nbsp;
join me in condemning violence and abuse against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:42.560
NHS staff.&nbsp; There was – there’s been recently&nbsp;
a high-profile incident at a GP practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:48.880
but we do know that this kind of behaviour is&nbsp;
suffered day in, day out by colleagues working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:54.160
in every part of the health service.&nbsp; Violence&nbsp;
and threatening behaviour are never okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.840
but particularly so when it’s against&nbsp;
people who come to work to help others.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.000
I hope that people feel that they do&nbsp;
have the tools they need to tackle this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.040
and we really would encourage people to report&nbsp;
incidences to the police.&nbsp; And they have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:14.640
securing literally hundreds of convictions a month&nbsp;
under the new legislation.&nbsp; But you know, really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:22.480
really want to make, I think, a plea to people&nbsp;
just to look after and continue to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:28.320
our NHS staff because we know that that is&nbsp;
something that means a great deal to the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:34.880
who are working within the NHS.&nbsp; But we have&nbsp;
to take clear action if that’s not the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.040
Finally, if I could just say this meeting&nbsp;
does fall just under a year on from the NHS’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:46.880
world‑leading commitment to reach net zero by 2045&nbsp;
and, of course, just weeks before the world comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:53.120
together in Glasgow for COP26.&nbsp; It’s a massive&nbsp;
achievement to be able to say that the NHS is on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:58.160
track to deliver its planned cut in emissions this&nbsp;
financial year.&nbsp; The emissions reduction this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:05.600
alone will be equivalent to that of 1.1 million&nbsp;
homes. &nbsp;So returning to the efficiency theme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.880
of course, energy-saving is also money-saving,&nbsp;
both now and in the long run.&nbsp; And we’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.280
just talking about the cost of monthly energy&nbsp;
bills, though the current volatility in energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:20.160
markets does show how using less and using&nbsp;
renewable energy does build resilience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:26.080
but it is the health impact too.&nbsp; The effects of&nbsp;
poor air quality and climate change are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.680
being seen in our GP practices, in our hospitals&nbsp;
and unfortunately in our mortuaries too.&nbsp; So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:37.200
whether it’s extreme heat waves or air pollution&nbsp;
causing asthma, heart disease, lung cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.000
there’s no doubt that this is a long-term&nbsp;
problem for the NHS.&nbsp; And it’s absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.960
right that we are part of the solution.&nbsp; But&nbsp;
we can’t do this alone, and that’s why it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:52.720
so important that we throw down the gauntlet today&nbsp;
to our suppliers as well.&nbsp; By wielding the massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:57.120
influence the NHS has as a buyer of goods and&nbsp;
services, we’re determined not just to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.840
better deal for taxpayers, but a better deal for&nbsp;
our environment and our future generations too.&nbsp;

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.120
Delivering on these commitments and many&nbsp;
others during the most difficult time in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.720
organisation’s history has only been possible&nbsp;
because of our staff and the many thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.560
volunteers and others who continue to support&nbsp;
them.&nbsp; Colleagues, I know you know this: it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:26.480
been tough, but there are also tough times ahead.&nbsp;
So, I will just end, if I may, my update by saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:32.960
thank you once again to every single person&nbsp;
who has worked for, who has supported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:37.600
the NHS over the last couple of years&nbsp;
and thank them again for the way they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.640
are rising to the challenges we now face.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Amanda, thank you very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:48.720
for that.&nbsp; What a wonderful introduction.&nbsp; I&nbsp;
wonder if I could just pick you up on one issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:53.200
and maybe Andrew Morris would like to come&nbsp;
in on this: you know, the absolute central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.200 --> 00:13:01.360
importance of our workforce, our hugely diverse&nbsp;
workforce, and how supportive we’re being to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:05.840
everybody who works in the NHS.&nbsp; And Andrew, I&nbsp;
know you’ve got some strong feelings on this.&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:15.040
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thank you, David.&nbsp; I mean, the&nbsp;
NHS employs 1.4 million people.&nbsp; We want to retain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:21.600
as many people as we can going into the winter and&nbsp;
for the future.&nbsp; Our plans are all predicated on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:30.800
filling the establishment to the maximum in order&nbsp;
to ensure recovery.&nbsp; So EDI, Equality, Diversity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:35.840
and Inclusion, is an agenda that’s really&nbsp;
important to the work of the People Committee.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:42.160
It’s central to being a good employer, and the&nbsp;
initiatives that we’re rolling out have improved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:48.160
retention rates quite markedly.&nbsp; And I know&nbsp;
Prerana has got a further series of measures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:54.400
we’ll be implementing over the coming months.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
it’s a central plank of the work we’re doing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.400 --> 00:14:00.800
I totally endorse the actions we’ve taken so far.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Andrew.&nbsp; And, Mike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:05.040
I know in the company that you used to run&nbsp;
at Sainsbury’s, that you put these issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.360
at absolutely the front of what you did there.
&gt;&gt;Mike Coupe: Yes, I think in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.760
modern front-facing organisation, and particularly&nbsp;
a big organisation like the NHS, it is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:20.320
critical for any number of reasons.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
Andrew’s already alluded to some of those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:25.680
whether it increases the talent pool from&nbsp;
which you’re drawing, it allows you to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:29.840
a diversity of perspective, increases the&nbsp;
diversity of skills that you can attract.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:36.160
To your employees, it shows that you trust and&nbsp;
you care, or that you should be trusted and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:40.640
care.&nbsp; And perhaps above and beyond anything&nbsp;
else, it actually mirrors the people that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:47.440
serve, because we live in a very ethnically&nbsp;
and diverse and inclusive community.&nbsp; So again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:52.480
I just reinforce Andrew’s point that the&nbsp;
Board is wholly behind the EDI initiative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.440 --> 00:15:00.160
that the team is driving through the organisation.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Mike.&nbsp; So both to Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:06.160
and to Prerana, you know, keep up the good work.&nbsp;&nbsp;
At the beginning of your introduction, Amanda, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:13.600
drew attention to the Galleri Grail cancer test,&nbsp;
you referred to the vaccination programme and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:19.040
the cholesterol-lowering injections that we’re&nbsp;
now introducing into the NHS.&nbsp; I’m just wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:24.160
whether, if Ara does – if Ara is – can hear&nbsp;
me all right, whether you’ve noticed a sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:30.000
of coming together of the life science, industry&nbsp;
and healthcare systems to improve healthcare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:38.320
So, Ara is, I think, temporary&nbsp;
dropped off the call.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:44.960
Anyway, Amanda, I think you see the life sciences&nbsp;
industry as playing a very big, important part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:49.040
how we deliver healthcare in the future.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:55.120
Yes, I think the last two years, as we’ve&nbsp;
responded to COVID, has really shown what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:03.680
can be done when we work in close partnership.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
that bench to bedside timescale can be compressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:10.720
as we know, down to not just years, to months,&nbsp;
to days.&nbsp; And I think what’s been great is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:15.120
see that energy continue over the summer with&nbsp;
these fantastic initiatives that we’ve been able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:19.600
to launch.&nbsp; And just a huge thank you again to&nbsp;
everybody who’s been involved, because these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:24.560
the life-saving interventions that we are going&nbsp;
to want to see more of in the future as well.&nbsp;

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:28.720
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And Amanda, look, you’ve&nbsp;
been in the health service for – I won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:33.120
embarrass you by saying how many years, but&nbsp;
you’ve worked at Chelsea and Westminster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:39.520
Guys and St Thomas’s.&nbsp; How – you know, so you’ve&nbsp;
seen what it’s like to be in a very pressurised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:45.040
acute hospital environment.&nbsp; How pressurised&nbsp;
do you feel the system is at the moment?&nbsp;

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:51.760
&gt;Amanda Pritchard: So, I think we have –&nbsp;
and honestly, the numbers tell the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:56.400
on this.&nbsp; But, you know, we’ve just had&nbsp;
the toughest summer the NHS has ever had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:03.040
coming off the back of the toughest 18 months&nbsp;
the NHS has ever had, responding to COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:09.120
And we’ve got a – I think we’ve got to be very&nbsp;
realistic, as I’ve just said, about what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:16.160
winter is likely to bring.&nbsp; But the thing that&nbsp;
has really struck me, David, is the level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:23.120
commitment and energy and determination across&nbsp;
particularly the leadership community in the NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:29.680
Because my sense, and I’ve asked my executive&nbsp;
colleagues their views as well, is that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:35.600
is a real determination from colleagues across&nbsp;
the whole NHS to say, ‘You know what? &nbsp;The last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:41.040
18 months have shown what the NHS can achieve,&nbsp;
it has risen to the challenge over and over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:48.640
over again.’&nbsp; And therefore, you know, there is a&nbsp;
can-do, we’ve proved we can do it.&nbsp; And this next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:54.480
challenge is going to be about being, you know,&nbsp;
deeply realistic about the scale of the challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.480 --> 00:18:00.240
but also, you know, deeply committed and&nbsp;
determined to continuing to tackle it in exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:06.880
the same level of determination, professionalism,&nbsp;
that care for each other, care for patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:12.480
that’s been shown over the last 18 months.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And maybe one last question.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:18.560
I mean, Tim, with Tim Ferris joining the team&nbsp;
now, I mean, you’ve been in the NHS for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:21.680
couple of months, your background’s always&nbsp;
been in the American care system.&nbsp; I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:27.840
how do you see – what are your first impressions,&nbsp;
having been over here now for two months, Tim?&nbsp;

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:34.160
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: David, that’s a terrific question&nbsp;
and thank you for the opportunity.&nbsp; I’ll just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:42.000
I spent my entire career at Mass General&nbsp;
Hospital in Boston and felt very fortunate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:52.560
work in an organisation with intense pride for&nbsp;
the organisation and one’s colleagues.&nbsp; And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:59.920
am – I feel incredibly fortunate I have landed&nbsp;
in a place that has just that intensity of pride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:07.600
in the work and in one’s colleagues.&nbsp;&nbsp;
That is an extraordinary force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:14.960
for the NHS and will bring us through a lot in&nbsp;
the future – all these pressures that Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:22.240
is talking about – by working together.&nbsp;
I’ll also say the NHS is an extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:33.520
place for innovation.&nbsp; And I mean, every time&nbsp;
I’ve been now out to – I’ve had 12 trust visits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:40.560
several practice visits and I’m looking forward to&nbsp;
many, many more.&nbsp; Every single place I have gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:47.680
there have been demonstrations of extraordinary&nbsp;
innovation, really cutting-edge innovation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:54.080
better ways to deliver care.&nbsp;
The challenge for us is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.080 --> 00:20:01.360
the energy and ideas around innovation; the&nbsp;
challenge is spreading them at scale and pace.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:07.440
And I just want to say to you, David, that Amanda&nbsp;
and the rest of the senior management team are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:15.600
really committed to doing that, to making sure&nbsp;
that where those improvements are, so that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:24.480
continue to reinforce the idea that doing your&nbsp;
job and working to improve your job are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:31.520
bring benefits not just to you and your patch,&nbsp;
but to the whole system.&nbsp; And I’m really proud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:40.000
and very pleased to be here and want to thank&nbsp;
you and Amanda and the Board for inviting me.&nbsp;

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:45.440
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Tim.&nbsp; So look, maybe&nbsp;
for the next public meeting that we have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:51.520
we could put innovation as a separate item on&nbsp;
the agenda and just showcase some of the actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:58.000
really remarkable innovations that are going on in&nbsp;
the NHS.&nbsp; So, we’ll put you up to do that, Tim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:03.200
Good.&nbsp; Well, Amanda, thank you very much&nbsp;
for that.&nbsp; I mean, you’ve made a great start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:07.760
and we’re fully behind you and look forward to&nbsp;
working with you as we go forward.&nbsp; So thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:13.120
And now we are moving on to Mark Cubbon,&nbsp;
who’s going to run us through the operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:18.640
performance.&nbsp; And Mark, I should say, is our&nbsp;
Interim Chief Operating Officer and was previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:24.000
Chief Executive at Portsmouth Hospital.&nbsp; Mark.
&gt;&gt;Mark Cubbon: Thank you very much.&nbsp; I’m going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:29.040
to briefly just introduce the performance report&nbsp;
and then hand over to colleagues, who will talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:34.160
through and give particular attention to&nbsp;
specific highlights within the report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:38.880
Amanda talked earlier about the context within&nbsp;
which our staff right across the service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:46.320
are responding day to day to the pandemic,&nbsp;
the current levels of the pandemic and indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.920
restoring services as quickly&nbsp;
as possible for our patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:57.920
At the moment, we have about 5,000 patients who&nbsp;
are in our beds with COVID.&nbsp; That number changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:02.240
from day to day, according to the level of&nbsp;
prevalence that we see in our community.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:08.480
We are seeing slightly more patients who require&nbsp;
access to critical care.&nbsp; So critical care has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:16.400
remained under continued pressure over the&nbsp;
past 18&nbsp;months, but our teams are working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:20.880
tremendously to deal with that pressure and to&nbsp;
balance the demands placed on their services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:28.000
We’re continuing to respond and to coordinate&nbsp;
the response as a level three incident.&nbsp; And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:33.200
means that our regional teams are taking the lead,&nbsp;
working with systems, working with organisations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:38.640
to provide the right coordinated effort to respond&nbsp;
to the day-to-day challenges and to support the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:46.320
recovery and restoration of our services.&nbsp; And,&nbsp;
of course, a continued focus and real attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:52.080
to infection prevention and control measures are&nbsp;
ensuring that we can continue to provide safe care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:57.520
to patients across our organisations.&nbsp;
I’m going to now hand over to Pauline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:04.000
who will talk through UEC and elective care and&nbsp;
touch on winter planning.&nbsp; And then I’m happy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:08.560
come back to talk through some other aspects of&nbsp;
the performance report after.&nbsp; Thank you, Chair.&nbsp;

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:15.360
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Thank you, Mark.&nbsp; So David, I&nbsp;
don’t want to repeat anything that Amanda said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:19.200
in her very comprehensive account, but&nbsp;
I’ll just highlight one or two points.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:25.280
And I think people have sight of the paper&nbsp;
as well, and so the detail is in there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:32.240
I’ll start off with just saying a little bit&nbsp;
about UEC, and first I feel I absolutely must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:37.120
acknowledge the wonderful work that our&nbsp;
staff continue to do day in, day out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:43.840
but also to acknowledge the fact that things have&nbsp;
not always been easy for our patients during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:51.200
summer period as the service struggled a bit more&nbsp;
than it does normally at that time of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:58.720
So basically, what happened over July, August&nbsp;
and September was demand returned to a level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:07.280
similar to what we saw in 2019/20.&nbsp; But at&nbsp;
the same time, we had capacity constraints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:12.640
because of what Amanda and Mark has&nbsp;
referred to, on the back of our COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:21.360
numbers, so with having about 6,000 patients&nbsp;
in beds, having to deal with the IPC – the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:28.240
necessary IPC measures – and because of staff&nbsp;
availability.&nbsp; And clearly, some of that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:34.320
down to the fact that our staff really did need&nbsp;
to have some holiday period during those months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:42.800
that there was very clear capacity issues.&nbsp;
I think we saw that most of all in the ambulance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:51.440
service, where the 999 service received&nbsp;
more calls than it had ever done previously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:58.720
and 111 calls were up by 39%, so quite a&nbsp;
significant hike in demand in both of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:04.720
areas.&nbsp; But just on the ambulance service alone,&nbsp;
to acknowledge that because of the great work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:10.160
of staff, the actual conveyancing numbers to&nbsp;
our emergency departments were actually less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:17.200
than we saw during the same period in 2019/20.&nbsp;
So what we’re doing – and it’s set out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:24.720
papers – is implementing a comprehensive recovery&nbsp;
plan for UEC and the ambulance service, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:32.080
that’ll be a fundamental part of the winter plan.&nbsp;
On elective, again to acknowledge the work that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:38.400
been done – Amanda referred to it earlier –&nbsp;
that activity returned to that 90% in July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:45.040
so that’s 90% of what we were doing in 2019/20.&nbsp;&nbsp;
At the same time, services throughout the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:52.640
are building their plans for how we provide care&nbsp;
to people who have been waiting the longest.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:59.280
Clearly, our first priority is always going to&nbsp;
be our P1 and P2 patients, our urgent patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:04.640
But we want to have a real focus, as Amanda&nbsp;
mentioned, to patients who have been waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:10.720
for more than two years, or indeed for anybody&nbsp;
who’s been waiting for over 52 weeks as well.&nbsp; So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:16.160
comprehensive plans being drawn up to be&nbsp;
implemented for the rest of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:24.480
At the same time, in order to maximise activity,&nbsp;
a real focus on transformation. &nbsp;And a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:32.000
of things highlighted in the report: greater use&nbsp;
of IS, the accelerator is paving the way for how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:37.840
we use transformation to increase activity, the&nbsp;
high volume/low complexity work, outpatient work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:45.920
the 17 early diagnostic units, and a lot of&nbsp;
work on the back of the clinically-based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:52.800
evidence-based interventions.&nbsp; And so, those&nbsp;
things are highlighted in the report.&nbsp; Thank you.&nbsp;

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:59.440
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Pauline.&nbsp; Before we open&nbsp;
it up for questions, I don’t know whether Keith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:03.040
Willett who I can see there,&nbsp;
who’s running our vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:08.320
programme now, would just like to come in?
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: Yes, thank you, David.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:14.080
perhaps I could just give a short overview of the&nbsp;
programme as it stands in the current context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:18.560
and then a little bit about the operational&nbsp;
complexity that we now have to deal with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:23.680
So, first of all, a big thank you to everybody&nbsp;
who works within the programme, and who indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:28.480
has come forward to take advantage of it.&nbsp; The&nbsp;
programme continues to make strong progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:35.760
delivering over 77 million vaccinations to&nbsp;
date.&nbsp; That’s over 40 million first doses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:43.040
37 million second doses.&nbsp; And for the most at&nbsp;
risk, that’s the Joint Committee on Vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:50.000
Immunisation cohorts, one to nine, that’s over&nbsp;
90% have received a vaccination.&nbsp; And for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:57.360
clinically extremely vulnerable adults, we’re now&nbsp;
at 94.6%. &nbsp;That’s quite remarkable as a nation.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:04.400
And in line with the guidance from the JCVI,&nbsp;
everyone aged over 16 is now able to book their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:08.720
doses on the national booking service,&nbsp;
in addition to receiving invitations from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:14.480
their general practice, choosing a community&nbsp;
pharmacy or finding an online walk-in centre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:21.200
People will also be aware that on 13th September,&nbsp;
the UK chief medical officers advised that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:27.520
children aged 12 to 15 will be offered one&nbsp;
dose of the Pfizer BioNTech COVID jab.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:32.560
And what – all that I’ve said there, I&nbsp;
just need to acknowledge at this point the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:37.040
increase in complexity of our vaccination&nbsp;
programme, we’ve now multiple different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:43.040
cohorts of citizens to be invited, different&nbsp;
vaccine types, different dosing schedules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:49.200
and indeed now four delivery models.&nbsp; So a&nbsp;
real thank you to the staff that are involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:55.040
In terms of the operational complexity, we&nbsp;
continue to provide the ‘evergreen’ offer.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:59.760
That’s for those who have been eligible for a&nbsp;
vaccine from the phase one and phase two of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:03.680
programme, but have yet to come forward.&nbsp; They’re&nbsp;
still very welcome to come forward, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:09.840
evergreen offer at those sites is still open.&nbsp;
The NHS is now, as people will be aware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:14.960
just starting to support the vaccination&nbsp;
rollout of the adult booster programme.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:20.240
That commenced on 16th September with the&nbsp;
first jab being administered to a maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:25.840
support worker in Croydon, and that was in 48&nbsp;
hours of the JCVI guidance being published.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:34.320
People will be invited when they become eligible,&nbsp;
and importantly JCVI advise that the booster jab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:38.640
should be no earlier than six months after&nbsp;
the second dose.&nbsp; That’s when it will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:44.640
most effective.&nbsp; So, the hospitals are already&nbsp;
offering booster vaccinations to their own staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:50.720
And important for the NHS and our wider care,&nbsp;
the national booking service is also now open to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:56.480
eligible health and social care workers to ensure&nbsp;
convenient and timely access for those working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.480 --> 00:30:02.560
in care homes, domiciliary care, as well as in&nbsp;
primary care, general practice and dental teams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:09.760
The JCVI also published guidance on a third&nbsp;
primary course vaccine dose for those individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:15.040
with suppressed immunity, and we started that&nbsp;
vaccination programme on 13th September.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:22.640
Delivery of that third dose is reliant on hospital&nbsp;
specialists and GPs to identify those eligible;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:28.960
and to determine with the patient the best time in&nbsp;
line with their treatment regime to receive that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:34.960
dose.&nbsp; As I mentioned earlier, the vaccination&nbsp;
programme is now open to all 12- to 15‑year‑olds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:40.080
and that’s underway.&nbsp; And it’s being delivered&nbsp;
primarily through our school-age immunisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:44.880
services.&nbsp; And that commenced last week.&nbsp;&nbsp;
That’s a new delivery model for the programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:51.760
and we’re following that progress carefully.&nbsp; In&nbsp;
addition, there was already present the at-risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:57.760
programme for the 12 to 15 year olds, who are&nbsp;
mainly been treated through primary care networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.760 --> 00:31:02.800
where those children are very well known.&nbsp;
And then finally, we are working still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:09.120
to increase the uptake amongst our 16- and&nbsp;
17-year-olds.&nbsp; Over 1,000 sites are now on online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:15.760
for the NHS walk-in option, and we’re looking to&nbsp;
improve the uptake in that group, which we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:21.040
are particularly vulnerable.&nbsp; Thank you, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Keith.&nbsp; And look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:25.920
on behalf of us all, Keith, thanks very much for&nbsp;
stepping up and taking responsibility for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:32.240
COVID programme after Emily sort of moved on.&nbsp; You&nbsp;
know, that was a really tough thing to do, but so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:37.840
you know, I know it’s taken, you know, seven days&nbsp;
a week, it’s been 24/7, so thank you very much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:41.680
I wonder whether I’ll come to Steve.&nbsp;
Just, Steve, if you’re looking forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:46.800
over the winter, thinking to just get your&nbsp;
view on where you think COVID and flu is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:52.080
going to take us through the next six months.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, thanks, David.&nbsp; So as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:57.840
I think Amanda pointed out, we still have&nbsp;
significant numbers, around 5,000 patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:03.120
in our hospital beds with COVID.&nbsp; Actually,&nbsp;
that is down over the course of September.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:10.000
I think we will be exiting September with about&nbsp;
20% less patients in our beds than we entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:16.880
in the beginning of September.&nbsp; So we’re now&nbsp;
back towards end of July/early August time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:23.280
So, of course, that is good news because that&nbsp;
is beds that are then largely available; not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:27.760
completely available because of the complexities&nbsp;
of keeping COVID and non-COVID patients apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:34.400
but are becoming more available to ease some of&nbsp;
the capacity pressures that we have seen.&nbsp; And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:38.480
of course, it’s great news, of course,&nbsp;
for everybody who’s at risk of COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:43.040
And that reflected infection rates have been&nbsp;
falling a bit in the communities, but we have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:48.800
infection rates in children start to rise.&nbsp; The&nbsp;
schools have gone back.&nbsp; That occurred in Scotland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:52.880
a bit earlier.&nbsp; Schools in Scotland go back&nbsp;
in the middle of August, although rates in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:56.960
Scotland have now started to come down.&nbsp;
So, it’s I think it’s an uncertain period.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.960 --> 00:33:01.360
And at this stage in the epidemic, I think our&nbsp;
epidemiological colleagues would say it’s quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:08.160
hard to model what the next few months will&nbsp;
look like.&nbsp; And, of course, the modelling that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:14.480
was done in the summer, actually the course of the&nbsp;
pandemic, has been at the optimistic end of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:21.440
but that means that it’s quite a wide range of&nbsp;
possibilities.&nbsp; All the more important then to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:26.560
keep the vaccine programme going at pace and&nbsp;
scale, as Keith has described, because those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:32.240
booster doses in particular, but also the 12 to&nbsp;
15 year old doses are going to be important going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.240 --> 00:33:35.520
forward in keeping infections under control.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And, of course, particularly in the boosters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:41.200
ensuring that any waning immunity that is going to&nbsp;
occur because that is the nature of vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:50.800
is countered in a timely way.&nbsp; So, I will add my&nbsp;
call to everybody to get the vaccine booster when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:55.200
you’re at that six-month point after your second&nbsp;
dose.&nbsp; That’s going to be critical going in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.840 --> 00:34:00.080
And then, of course, flu, we didn’t see much flu&nbsp;
last year, although we did have a fantastic flu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:04.880
vaccine programme last year, which was the best&nbsp;
ever.&nbsp; But this year, it needs to be better even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.880 --> 00:34:13.040
again, because again there is uncertainty around&nbsp;
flu.&nbsp; I think we are concerned clearly that flu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:18.720
not having been around much last winter,&nbsp;
will come back at a higher level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:23.840
so we’re already keeping a watchful eye on that.&nbsp;
And then other respiratory infections such as RSV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:28.560
which is an infection that normally affects&nbsp;
young children but can affect all ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:36.160
we’ve seen some unseasonable spikes in that&nbsp;
over the summer, because everything is so out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.160 --> 00:34:39.760
of kilter because of the unusual 18 months&nbsp;
we’ve had, so another close eye on that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:48.000
And then of course, we are seeing people coming&nbsp;
back who have delayed seeking healthcare and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:53.600
now coming forward both to our elective services&nbsp;
and in some cases, unfortunately, to our emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:58.560
services.&nbsp; So, it’s not a normal winter, it&nbsp;
won’t be a normal winter.&nbsp; There’s a range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:04.480
of uncertainty there, and all the more reason&nbsp;
for the planning that Pauline has described,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:11.040
and for the work that Keith and others are doing,&nbsp;
so that we can ensure the public is as protected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:16.000
as possible as we go into the winter months.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Steve.&nbsp; So Andrew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.560
then Amanda.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:25.680
Thanks, David.&nbsp; Can I ask Keith if JVCI have&nbsp;
pronounced whether you can do a flu jab and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:31.360
booster jab simultaneously?&nbsp; Because I’m just&nbsp;
thinking about, you know, the immense workload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:37.200
that your team is undertaking, and clearly&nbsp;
some clarity on this would enormously help.&nbsp;

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:40.800
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: Andrew, thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Yes, the answer is yes, you can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:47.520
and we have promoted that where it is&nbsp;
convenient for the individual, the patient;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:51.040
where it’s convenient and efficient&nbsp;
for the system.&nbsp; But what I’d also say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.680 --> 00:35:56.560
equally important is that we’re co-promoting&nbsp;
it.&nbsp; So, if someone turns up for one vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.560 --> 00:36:01.120
as Steve says, we very much raise the profile,&nbsp;
‘Well, why don’t you have the other one as well?’&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:08.160
But also, an important aspect is where the&nbsp;
programmes are sequenced in some way for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:13.680
variety of reasons, it’s important that you don’t&nbsp;
wait for one until the other one is available.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:19.040
So, if you’re offered flu, take it now; and when&nbsp;
your COVID turn comes, take the COVID then.&nbsp; Don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:24.880
wait to hold the two together, because we don’t&nbsp;
quite know what the transmission is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.880 --> 00:36:28.400
and the winter looks like.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, thanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:32.400
Keith.&nbsp; Amanda, then Wol.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:37.280
Thank you, David. I just want, just because&nbsp;
I just wanted to make sure we called it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:43.200
and I know we’ve covered a whole load of stuff&nbsp;
already.&nbsp; But in the report, I think Mark has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:49.040
particularly picked up some of the data on&nbsp;
where we are with mental health services.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:55.840
And I think it would just be – I just wanted&nbsp;
to say, I think number one, to acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:59.440
that the pressures that we’re seeing on all parts&nbsp;
of the system are no different on mental health.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:04.160
In fact, in some parts of our mental health&nbsp;
services, particularly children and young people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:09.600
they are under even more acute pressure,&nbsp;
actually, than some other parts of the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:17.280
But secondly, just to say how important I&nbsp;
think it remains that we continue to have our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.280 --> 00:37:23.040
commitment to the long-term planned goals around&nbsp;
mental health services.&nbsp; And I spent yesterday in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:29.440
Bradford visiting school, visiting various&nbsp;
different parts of the system.&nbsp; And one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:35.440
the things that came across so strongly from that&nbsp;
visit was also just how important it is that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:41.440
is provided in that integrated partnership way.&nbsp;
So the act as one work in Bradford brings together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:48.160
in a local community, voluntary sector, schools,&nbsp;
health, health professionals, health volunteers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:55.120
and they really genuinely do act as one around&nbsp;
the services they’re providing, with a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.760 --> 00:38:01.680
lens on reducing inequalities.&nbsp; So, for me, it was&nbsp;
just a really important thing to highlight in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:07.280
conversation, that a) we recognise that there’s&nbsp;
real pressure on mental health services; b)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:13.600
that long-term plan, commitment remains, you&nbsp;
know, fundamentally important; and c) we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:19.840
seeing – again it’s Tim’s point earlier – some&nbsp;
just brilliant examples of innovation partnership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:25.200
working all across the country.&nbsp; And certainly,&nbsp;
that is as true in mental health as anywhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:29.840
and it just deserves, I think, acknowledging.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No, I’m really glad you said that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:34.240
Amanda.&nbsp; Mental health is so&nbsp;
important.&nbsp; Wol, then Ruth.&nbsp;

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:40.080
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you, David.&nbsp; Just&nbsp;
a quick question on the vaccinations.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:45.680
Keith, you just gave an excellent articulation&nbsp;
of what’s going on and the complexity.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:50.080
I’m wondering, given the lessons we learnt&nbsp;
earlier on about getting to hard-to-reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:54.240
communities and thinking about the extra work&nbsp;
we’re going to do on the booster programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.800 --> 00:39:00.160
because this isn’t so much, you know, when they&nbsp;
arrive, giving them both flu and the co-vaccine;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:05.520
it’s more getting them there in the first place&nbsp;
and ensuring we, at pace, get to a point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:10.960
– you know, when the high 70s 80s 90s, if you&nbsp;
like.&nbsp; And then really focus on that, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:16.560
the ethnically diverse, the issue we had around&nbsp;
non-health seeking behaviour by some communities.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:20.000
What are we going do about that, please?
&gt;&gt;Keith Willett: Yes, thank you, Wol.&nbsp; We’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:24.560
learned an awful lot, haven’t we,&nbsp;
about inequalities, deprivation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:28.800
what I call the failing-to-reach groups rather&nbsp;
than the hard-to-reach because it is our failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:34.720
if you like, that we haven’t connected.&nbsp; And the&nbsp;
more and more we understand, the more complex it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:41.520
gets and the more local and specific it becomes.&nbsp;
Working through local communities, what we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:46.560
calling the hyper-local focus, is absolutely&nbsp;
critical.&nbsp; And that’s right down to making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:51.840
sure that the people who are talking about the&nbsp;
vaccination and promoting it are from that group;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:56.000
that they are the faith leaders, they&nbsp;
are the local community leaders, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:01.680
are the respected.&nbsp; They may be the influencers&nbsp;
in social media or just local influencers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:07.440
and those are the people we need to get to first&nbsp;
because they carry the communities with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:12.320
And there is, unfortunately, a lot of&nbsp;
misinformation out there that does need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:17.360
addressed.&nbsp; And that’s not something government&nbsp;
and, arguably, senior NHS leaders can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:23.360
Certainly, the NHS frontline staff are very&nbsp;
trusted, but it’s local communities who are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.360 --> 00:40:28.640
focus.&nbsp; And I think we’ve done the big steps.&nbsp;&nbsp;
We’ve even done, I think, the hard yards.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:35.600
In this, now, we’re right down to individual&nbsp;
focus, to individual people, to have them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:41.440
come alongside the vaccination programme.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
it is critical, because we know that, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:46.080
an older person or a vulnerable person who&nbsp;
hasn’t been vaccinated is at considerable risk.&nbsp;

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:51.200
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Keith.&nbsp; We’ll go to&nbsp;
Ruth; and then Julian, we’ll come to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:55.440
if we can, for the financial report.&nbsp; So, Ruth.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you, David.&nbsp; I just want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.440 --> 00:41:00.320
just build a little bit on what Pauline and&nbsp;
Amanda have said, in that the pressure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:07.280
absolutely in our hospitals and our community&nbsp;
services, but also in our maternity units.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:14.800
And I’m very grateful for the nurses and midwives&nbsp;
across all of the sectors.&nbsp; I was in Liverpool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.800 --> 00:41:20.640
Her Majesty’s Prison in Liverpool,&nbsp;
witnessing some amazing nursing leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:25.920
particularly what they were telling me through the&nbsp;
first wave of this pandemic.&nbsp; And also, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:32.640
those particular small services like the homeless&nbsp;
and the inclusion nurses at Beacon House in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:37.360
Colchester.&nbsp; They’ve done an amazing piece&nbsp;
of work, even though the pressures are such.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.080 --> 00:41:45.520
I guess for me, it will be the ways that teams&nbsp;
have come together during this pandemic response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:52.080
that I will be the most proud of.&nbsp; And I was&nbsp;
lucky enough to go to LAS Ambulance Service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:57.120
last week and meet the mental health&nbsp;
nurses working with paramedics as one team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:03.840
the consultant midwife working with the paramedics&nbsp;
as one team.&nbsp; And it’s really great to see that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:08.320
So, the supply of nursing is going to be&nbsp;
important, and we’re doing some great work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:13.600
on that.&nbsp; And HEE is doing some amazing work&nbsp;
with Mark Redford and team on the supply of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:18.720
future nurses.&nbsp; And Duncan Burton and team are&nbsp;
doing some amazing work with trusts across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:25.216
country on international recruitment.&nbsp; But it’s&nbsp;
now how we support our nurses and NHS staff that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.216 --> 00:42:31.600
is going to be important, as well as recognising.&nbsp;
And my final point, I just want to congratulate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:39.760
if I may, the Windrush Generation&nbsp;
Committee and Whittington hospital because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:45.760
just earlier this month, they celebrated&nbsp;
Windrush Generation and their commitment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:45.760 --> 00:42:50.640
and their contribution to the NHS.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
it was a wonderful way to celebrate nurses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:55.840
across England.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.840 --> 00:43:02.480
That’s very inspirational, Ruth.&nbsp; Thank you very&nbsp;
much for that.&nbsp; Less inspirational perhaps, moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:11.360
on to the finance report from Julian.&nbsp; Julian.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:17.680
Sorry, I was struggling with the technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Look, I won’t repeat what is set out in the paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.680 --> 00:43:27.280
other than to say we are living within the budget&nbsp;
that was agreed for the first half of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:37.200
It included £1 billion provided for elective&nbsp;
recovery, which has largely been both drawn down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.200 --> 00:43:41.920
and spent; and has been critical to delivering&nbsp;
the levels of extra elective care that Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:51.120
referred to at the very beginning of this&nbsp;
meeting, as trusts continued to live with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.320 --> 00:43:57.840
the sort of operational constraints&nbsp;
and demands that arise from COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.480 --> 00:44:02.640
Worth saying that today we have published the&nbsp;
planning guidance for the second half of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:02.640 --> 00:44:12.160
following agreement of funding with government&nbsp;
for the second half of the year.&nbsp; It basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:20.560
allows us to maintain cash levels of funding&nbsp;
into the NHS, it continues to provide £1&nbsp;billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.240 --> 00:44:27.840
another £1 billion of support for elective&nbsp;
recovery over the second half of the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.840 --> 00:44:36.080
as we seek to maintain and increase services&nbsp;
alongside normal additional winter demands.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:42.160
It means we can continue with the hospital&nbsp;
discharge programme, which has been so critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:51.280
to enabling us to maintain flow through hospitals&nbsp;
and the capacity of hospitals.&nbsp; It will enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.280 --> 00:44:58.560
us to continue the same scheme for mental health&nbsp;
services throughout the second half of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:08.000
It also sets out the details for how&nbsp;
we are looking to spend £500 million of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:12.000
capital and around £200 million&nbsp;
of revenue support that, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:14.400
the government have provided&nbsp;
for the second half of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:22.960
that will enable us to look at how we can further&nbsp;
increase our capacity to deal with the backlog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.960 --> 00:45:32.000
of care in the acute settings; and look at how&nbsp;
we can invest in technology and transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:37.440
that again, will be – will put the NHS in a&nbsp;
better place to both deal with driving out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:45.840
sustainable financial savings and improved&nbsp;
performance as we look ahead to 2022, 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:46.960 --> 00:45:52.800
Worth saying that in the plan for the&nbsp;
second half of the year, we are asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.800 --> 00:46:03.120
providers to deliver a 2% efficiency saving on&nbsp;
average as we continue to restore normal financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:11.280
disciplines, as we have come through COVID.&nbsp;
So, that’s where we are.&nbsp; We’re clearly still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:18.400
in the process of some of the spending review&nbsp;
negotiations.&nbsp; In particular, that will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.400 --> 00:46:24.800
critical to setting the right capital budget for&nbsp;
the NHS; and a really, really important factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:32.880
that we get that right, so that we can deliver the&nbsp;
ambitions the government set out for elective care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:36.880
at the beginning of September.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.880 --> 00:46:41.440
Thanks, Julian.&nbsp; Well, clearly the investment&nbsp;
in the workforce is going to be crucial to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:41.440 --> 00:46:48.640
this.&nbsp; And I just wondered, David Behan, given&nbsp;
your chairmanship of HEE, how do you see that?&nbsp;

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:54.640
&gt;&gt;David Behan: Thanks, David.&nbsp; And thanks,&nbsp;
Julian.&nbsp; So, I think this is a challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.200 --> 00:47:05.680
time for workforce, but the good news is that&nbsp;
I think the work that HEE is doing with NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:12.560
Improvement, NHS England as well as the Department&nbsp;
of Health, so sorting this on a trilateral basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.560 --> 00:47:19.200
is absolutely fundamental to the future. &nbsp;And I&nbsp;
just pay tribute to Julian and the way that he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.200 --> 00:47:24.400
worked with Navina and Calum in HEE and with the&nbsp;
department.&nbsp; And I think there’s more work that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:33.120
need to do as we go into the autumn, to actually&nbsp;
be clear that we do have sufficient to meet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:37.440
baseline commitments, but also the resources there&nbsp;
to actually make some of the transformational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.440 --> 00:47:45.760
changes we want to make.&nbsp; And again, particularly&nbsp;
important that the work that we’re doing in HEE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:51.840
with Prerana and through the People Boards, and&nbsp;
the People Advisory committee, which Andrew Morris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:58.960
co-chairs with me, are the vehicles to do this.&nbsp;
But I think we need to be clear and honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.960 --> 00:48:02.720
David, that there are some challenges when&nbsp;
we look at the workforce going forward.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:09.360
we’re working through those and&nbsp;
we will come up with a clear plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:10.960 --> 00:48:17.600
later, during October.&nbsp; But there are some choices&nbsp;
to be made.&nbsp; And what choices mean is that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.600 --> 00:48:21.360
mean we will be able to do some things and not&nbsp;
do everything that everybody wants us to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:26.480
And that’s not a great place to be, but we do&nbsp;
need to make some decisions about priorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:26.480 --> 00:48:31.840
But the important thing, I think, is that we’re&nbsp;
doing that collectively and collaboratively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:36.720
and not doing that separately in isolation.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And that’s where we can ensure for the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.720 --> 00:48:40.160
we have got, we’ll get the best value&nbsp;
for that money, because it’s an agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:40.160 --> 00:48:43.920
set of investments that we will make, but&nbsp;
thanks for the opportunity just to say that.&nbsp;

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:48.320
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, David.&nbsp; And of&nbsp;
course, I mean, technology is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:55.920
so important as we go forward.&nbsp; And I just&nbsp;
wonder, Laura or Simon Bolton from NHS Digital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:55.920 --> 00:49:01.440
whether you’d like to comment at all?
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: Well, thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:10.320
David.&nbsp; I would sort of echo what David&nbsp;
Behan said in terms of the trilateral working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:17.680
to make sure that as we look at the&nbsp;
next few years, we’ve got the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:23.200
investment in tech to actually&nbsp;
create the efficiencies and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.200 --> 00:49:28.880
create the changes that we’re looking for.&nbsp;
So, I think we obviously will have an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:38.240
kind of request in terms of the spending review,&nbsp;
because I think some of that is we still do need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:44.720
to up our level of investment in some of the&nbsp;
underlying technology support.&nbsp; But I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:49.920
sort of endorse what David said in terms of the&nbsp;
way we’re approaching that is hugely important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.920 --> 00:50:00.560
And I think the opportunity is what Amanda was&nbsp;
articulating, which is as we seek to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:06.480
the current challenges, how do we do it in ways&nbsp;
that actually build for the future?&nbsp; And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:11.440
part of that is our use of digital and tech.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Laura.&nbsp; And it’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:16.720
good to have Simon Bolton with us today, Simon&nbsp;
being the Interim Chief Executive of NHS Digital.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:17.760 --> 00:50:22.720
Simon, did you want to comment at all?
&gt;&gt;Simon Bolton: Yeah, if I may, David.&nbsp; I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:22.720 --> 00:50:27.760
think just to build on those comments, I’ve been&nbsp;
in the role for about four months now.&nbsp; And I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:35.120
really delighted at the way that the central teams&nbsp;
are coming together across EIDNX[?], to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:35.120 --> 00:50:42.480
really deeply about what we can do to support the&nbsp;
providers and the ICSs to really make a difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:47.920
with digital technology.&nbsp; Because, of course,&nbsp;
this has to be a partnership, making sure that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.640 --> 00:50:54.000
prioritise the things that are most important to&nbsp;
the service and work together to deliver those.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:54.000 --> 00:51:00.880
So, it’s really delightful to see that those teams&nbsp;
are coming together.&nbsp; So, I think that’s all I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.880 --> 00:51:07.600
add at this stage.&nbsp; Plenty of work to do, though.&nbsp;
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay.&nbsp; Thanks, Simon.&nbsp; And Julian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:11.600
just on behalf of us all, you know, a&nbsp;
huge thank you to you and your team for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:12.400 --> 00:51:17.200
spending the whole of your summer holidays working&nbsp;
on the financial settlement that we’ve got,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.200 --> 00:51:24.320
and we hope that you’ll get a good&nbsp;
settlement on 27th&nbsp;October because capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.320 --> 00:51:29.280
and, of course, workforce are both hugely&nbsp;
important.&nbsp; So, Julian, thank you very much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:30.240 --> 00:51:36.720
So, shall we move on then to the next item,&nbsp;
which is delivery of net zero NHS?&nbsp; Can I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:39.500
Nick Watts on the call?&nbsp; Nick?&nbsp;
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: I’m here.&nbsp; Hi, David.&nbsp;

00:51:39.500 --> 00:51:44.320
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hello, Nick.&nbsp; Nick, well look, I&nbsp;
think, maybe Ian, you’d like to introduce Nick.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:44.320 --> 00:51:49.120
But I’ll just say that I’ve been working with Nick&nbsp;
closely for the last year or so, and you’ve done a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:49.120 --> 00:51:54.560
phenomenal job in really putting this at the top&nbsp;
when it’s one of our top priorities in the NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.560 --> 00:51:59.120
But Ian, do you just want to kick it off?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: You’ve given a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:59.120 --> 00:52:05.280
intro to Nick, so straight over to Nick.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: All right, thank you.&nbsp; Listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:11.840
the entirety of everything that we have achieved&nbsp;
over the last 12 months, that’s outlined in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:11.840 --> 00:52:18.960
paper.&nbsp; Except that it’s not, right?&nbsp; Except&nbsp;
to try to capture the energy, everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:24.800
that is happening across the NHS to respond to&nbsp;
climate change – not possible in just a paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:24.800 --> 00:52:33.600
not possible in just a 25-minute Board item. &nbsp;
Nonetheless, one year ago almost to the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.560 --> 00:52:38.560
we became, the NHS became the world’s&nbsp;
first healthcare system to commit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.560 --> 00:52:46.480
net zero emissions.&nbsp; The words I think I’ve most&nbsp;
frequently heard used to describe that strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:52.160
to canter alongside it, is a combination of ‘wow,&nbsp;
that’s ambitious’, and also, ‘it’s evidence-based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:52.160 --> 00:52:56.240
it’s feasible, right?’&nbsp; Ambitious, but&nbsp;
feasible, which I think is precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.240 --> 00:53:03.200
the balance there that we were trying to strike.
It came together as a result of an expert panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:03.200 --> 00:53:07.680
which brought together health professionals,&nbsp;
climatologists, engineers from across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:07.680 --> 00:53:12.000
the very best we have, but also a public&nbsp;
consultation and a consultation with our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:17.840
staff.&nbsp; And I remind everyone I speak to. &nbsp;Every&nbsp;
single time you go out and you ask our 1.4 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:23.840
staff – and we did this just a month ago – ‘Where&nbsp;
do you want to see us invest our time, invest our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:29.280
attention?’ nine out of ten of them – and&nbsp;
this is despite everything that’s happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:34.640
across the world over the last 12 months.&nbsp; Nine&nbsp;
out of ten shout back, ‘We want to see the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:34.640 --> 00:53:39.840
we want to work with the NHS as we drive, as&nbsp;
we deliver a response to climate change.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.200 --> 00:53:44.400
So, 2040 for our direct emissions was our target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:50.400
2045 for the entire footprint, including&nbsp;
our supply chain.&nbsp; To put that in context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:58.640
that’s 1,260 kilotonnes of carbon we were&nbsp;
aiming to reduce over the last 12 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.640 --> 00:54:04.480
Amanda, you put that in even better context: 1.1&nbsp;
million homes – the equivalent of powering 1.1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.480 --> 00:54:10.240
million homes across the country, 1.7 million&nbsp;
flights from London to New York and back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:16.480
It’s been 12 months, and there’s been&nbsp;
a lot that’s happened.&nbsp; In our Estates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.480 --> 00:54:24.480
Simon Corben and his team working to deliver&nbsp;
investments of over £310 million across 94 trusts:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:24.480 --> 00:54:28.000
smarter, more efficient Estates.&nbsp;&nbsp;
A lot of what you want to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:31.840
to respond to climate change, it has some of&nbsp;
the best returns on investment you will see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:31.840 --> 00:54:35.120
paying back in two to three years because&nbsp;
it’s all about increasing efficiency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.880 --> 00:54:43.760
This comes and accompanies a new net-zero hospital&nbsp;
standard, but it also looks like real things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:43.760 --> 00:54:50.160
happening on the ground: 2,500 solar panels on the&nbsp;
roof of Milton Keynes Hospital, modern heat pumps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:55.520
at Gloucestershire hospital, saving money that&nbsp;
can then be reinvested back into the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.920 --> 00:55:02.000
James Cook and his team leading a lot of&nbsp;
our work on travel and transport, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:04.480
agreed at the last Board meeting a trajectory to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:05.120 --> 00:55:11.840
net zero – our non-emergency patient transport.&nbsp;&nbsp;
Already, we have seen parts of the system respond:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:16.400
South Central Ambulance Service, starting to&nbsp;
purchase zero-emission rapid response vehicles;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.120 --> 00:55:21.840
Oxford University Hospital using&nbsp;
electric bikes for couriers to deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:28.080
cancer treatment across communities that&nbsp;
they serve; the world’s first I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:35.280
just recently quite a large 18-tonne&nbsp;
truck – electric trucks used to move goods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:42.160
around the service for the Northern Care Alliance;&nbsp;
and if we can keep quiet about it on this public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:47.440
Board, sometime in a couple of weeks we hope to&nbsp;
be able to be in a position to talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.440 --> 00:55:54.000
world’s first electric ambulances that are able to&nbsp;
supply both our urban and our rural populations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:55.840 --> 00:56:02.400
In the world of medicines 25% of our emissions,&nbsp;
pharmaceuticals make up.&nbsp; Blake Dark – I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:05.920
he’s somewhere on this call; Claire Foreman have&nbsp;
been leading much of that work.&nbsp; It takes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.920 --> 00:56:10.240
form of the national over-prescribing&nbsp;
review, demonstrating the one-in-one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:10.240 --> 00:56:14.800
hand‑in‑hand alignment of net zero in response&nbsp;
to climate change and better patient care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:22.880
We set targets for a 10% use of desflurane, a&nbsp;
particularly highly polluting anaesthetic gas; 10%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:22.880 --> 00:56:28.160
is what we wanted to hit.&nbsp; We blew past that, and&nbsp;
trusts are now going further and faster.&nbsp; In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:32.800
I was on calls just the other day.&nbsp; People weren’t&nbsp;
talking about 10% or 5%; they wanted to eliminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:36.400
it altogether.&nbsp; And they think they can do&nbsp;
that while improving care for their patients.&nbsp;

00:56:39.280 --> 00:56:44.560
We’ve also seen – and so it was 12 months&nbsp;
ago we released our own net zero strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:45.760 --> 00:56:50.800
Despite everything that’s been happening, 155&nbsp;
trusts across the country have almost completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:50.800 --> 00:56:55.360
their own localised, internalised,&nbsp;
net zero strategy.&nbsp; Every trust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:55.360 --> 00:57:00.400
by the end of the financial year, we’re going to&nbsp;
have with their own local strategy.&nbsp; A green plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:00.400 --> 00:57:04.000
and that’s where I think we need the help of&nbsp;
this Board to make sure that that message is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:09.360
clear that those net zero strategies are built&nbsp;
into our integrated care systems as they come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:13.200
life, built into the work that our trusts are&nbsp;
doing as they recover and respond to COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:18.720
All of those things together, estates,&nbsp;
medicines, the way that we deliver our care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.720 --> 00:57:21.520
the way that we move our patients and&nbsp;
work with our patients across the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.720 --> 00:57:28.960
puts us on track to deliver the reductions that&nbsp;
we were talking about: 1,260 kilotonnes of carbon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.960 --> 00:57:35.840
12 months on.&nbsp; It’s unprecedented to have the NHS&nbsp;
be the world’s first healthcare system to commit.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:36.720 --> 00:57:41.520
It’s even more impressive to be able to say that&nbsp;
we were able to deliver against that one year on.&nbsp;

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:48.160
That’s the first half of that paper, but&nbsp;
there’s something equally as important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:48.160 --> 00:57:54.000
and it’s the work that Preeya Bailie and her team&nbsp;
have been doing.&nbsp; She’s the author of the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:54.000 --> 00:58:01.520
half of this paper, but not here today with us.&nbsp;
Within the decade, the NHS will no longer purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.520 --> 00:58:06.080
from anyone who doesn’t meet or exceed our&nbsp;
commitments on net zero.&nbsp; This is the words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.080 --> 00:58:10.960
from our net zero strategy 12 months ago.&nbsp; Now,&nbsp;
we knew that we needed to go away and we needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:10.960 --> 00:58:16.240
to work with suppliers to make sure that we were&nbsp;
developing a pipeline and a pathway to net zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:16.240 --> 00:58:21.680
that work both for the NHS and for the industry.&nbsp;
Preeya and her team, they’ve consulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:21.680 --> 00:58:26.080
extensively, they’ve started innovation&nbsp;
competitions to broaden the pipeline, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.080 --> 00:58:30.640
the response that has come back is absolutely ‘we&nbsp;
are there with you’.&nbsp; David, you’ve been leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:35.280
an international leadership group.&nbsp; Since we&nbsp;
started this journey already, Johnson &amp; Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:40.960
Smith and Nephew, GSK, AstraZeneca, at least&nbsp;
some of our largest suppliers have come online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:46.000
and said, ‘Absolutely, we want to work with&nbsp;
the NHS to get to net zero.’&nbsp; And so the paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:47.680 --> 00:58:52.880
takes this journey one step further.&nbsp;
From April 2022, we’re proposing 10%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:58.640
of all procurement decisions start to consider&nbsp;
a net zero weighting, and indeed we launch a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:58.640 --> 00:59:04.400
sustainable supplier framework to work with our&nbsp;
80,000 suppliers on this journey.&nbsp; April 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.400 --> 00:59:10.080
all contracts over £5 million start to&nbsp;
demonstrate their alignment with our ambitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:16.720
for the direct emissions.&nbsp; And April 2027, all&nbsp;
contracts full stop across all scopes starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:18.080 --> 00:59:21.360
develop and demonstrate their alignment&nbsp;
with our ambitions.&nbsp; And that is how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:21.360 --> 00:59:27.440
for our direct emissions and for our entire&nbsp;
footprint, we get to net zero by 2040 and 2045.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.800 --> 00:59:34.640
So, listen, I’ll stop there.&nbsp; There is so&nbsp;
much more that I could say.&nbsp; There is so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:34.640 --> 00:59:41.440
so much to be proud of, so much that has happened&nbsp;
while there has been so much else going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:41.440 --> 00:59:48.720
across the country.&nbsp; It is so exciting to be able&nbsp;
to demonstrate not only is delivering a net zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:48.720 --> 00:59:53.360
healthcare system possible, but it’s exciting.&nbsp;&nbsp;
It’s good for staff, it’s good for our patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:53.360 --> 00:59:56.080
and with your support, it’s inevitable.
Thank you.&nbsp;

00:59:56.080 --> 01:00:00.640
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Nick, thank you very&nbsp;
much for that.&nbsp; Looking at Mike&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:06.480
Coupe, who I know has a keen interest in&nbsp;
sustainability, what are your reflections&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.480 --> 01:00:12.560
on the programme that has just been outlined?
&gt;&gt;Mike Coupe: Well, first of all, the excitement&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:12.560 --> 01:00:19.040
is very palpable.&nbsp; And to the point that’s already&nbsp;
being made, not only is it a societal expectation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.040 --> 01:00:23.120
it’s also an expectation of our employees&nbsp;
and indeed our future employees.&nbsp; So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:23.120 --> 01:00:28.880
it’s absolutely fantastic, the work that we’re&nbsp;
doing.&nbsp; I don’t underestimate the challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:34.080
I suspect we probably know half the answers to&nbsp;
the questions, but to your point about ambition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:34.080 --> 01:00:37.760
we probably don’t know the other half of the&nbsp;
questions that we need to answer over the period&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:37.760 --> 01:00:44.000
of time that we’re undertaking the work.&nbsp; But I&nbsp;
think what you’re doing is absolutely amazing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.000 --> 01:00:51.520
clearly front and centre of the world’s biggest&nbsp;
challenge as we look forward, so goes way beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.320 --> 01:00:56.560
you know, the sort of most immediate time&nbsp;
horizons that we tend to talk about.&nbsp; So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:56.560 --> 01:01:00.560
congratulations, and I think you bring a lot&nbsp;
of energy to the work, and it’s great to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:00.560 --> 01:01:05.520
the organisation working the way that it is.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Mike.&nbsp; And I wondered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.520 --> 01:01:09.600
Nick, whether you could just give a few&nbsp;
reflections on COP26, which is now, what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:12.560
only six weeks away or something, or even less?
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:22.000
Close, close, terrifyingly close.&nbsp; COP26, five&nbsp;
years on it marks from the world coming together&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:28.960
to sign the Paris Agreement, a global agreement&nbsp;
to limit temperature rise to well below 2 degrees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:28.960 --> 01:01:36.800
1.5 degrees, roughly where science thinks the&nbsp;
safe threshold is.&nbsp; It’s in about four weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:37.920 --> 01:01:43.120
It’s important to the NHS, I think, for a&nbsp;
couple of reasons.&nbsp; We made our commitment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.120 --> 01:01:46.960
12&nbsp;months ago.&nbsp; Since then, we have been&nbsp;
joined by our friends across the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:54.160
President&nbsp;Biden announced only a couple of weeks&nbsp;
ago the formation of a new Climate Change and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:59.760
Health Equity unit, a department within HHS&nbsp;
looking to build off the foundations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:06.560
the NHS has developed.&nbsp; In Germany, there’s a new&nbsp;
department springing into life within the Federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:06.560 --> 01:02:10.560
Ministry of Health focused on the response to&nbsp;
climate change, both mitigation and adaptation.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:19.280
I think now in six out of six states in&nbsp;
Australia, they have their own equivalents&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:19.280 --> 01:02:24.640
working on this as well, coming online in Western&nbsp;
Australia and New South Wales, Queensland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:28.400
The reason I mention all of this, David,&nbsp;
is this is the moment where the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:33.280
comes together to make some of these joint&nbsp;
commitments.&nbsp; And it’s quite exciting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.280 --> 01:02:37.440
us because we are seeing increasingly that&nbsp;
healthcare systems all around the world are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:37.440 --> 01:02:41.280
starting to follow the leadership from the&nbsp;
NHS and indeed are forging their own paths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.400 --> 01:02:47.840
I say again and again, the NHS can’t get to&nbsp;
net zero unless we all go there together:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:47.840 --> 01:02:52.960
all 1.4&nbsp;million of our staff, all of our suppliers&nbsp;
– all 80,000 of them – and healthcare systems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:53.920 --> 01:02:58.480
we are intertwined with across the&nbsp;
world.&nbsp; The reason COP is so important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:58.480 --> 01:03:03.120
the UN Climate Change summit, is because it’s the&nbsp;
moment where we reaffirm and we demonstrate again&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:03.120 --> 01:03:07.840
and again that actually we’re not alone; actually,&nbsp;
everyone is moving this way with us because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:07.840 --> 01:03:12.000
evidence is pretty clear.&nbsp; The response to&nbsp;
climate change is good for health, it is good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:17.920
for our patients, it is good for the NHS.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And I should add, Laura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:17.920 --> 01:03:23.600
that this is just another example where&nbsp;
digital health, virtual health can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:23.600 --> 01:03:28.080
a huge contribution.&nbsp; I think I’m right in&nbsp;
saying, Nick, that something like 20% of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:28.080 --> 01:03:34.160
traffic journeys at rush hour in Birmingham are&nbsp;
health-related.&nbsp; You know, if we can just get cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:34.160 --> 01:03:40.000
off the road, it will make a huge difference.&nbsp;
I’m wondering, Wol, in your world that you – that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:46.320
you’re in, is climate change, sustainability&nbsp;
becoming, you know, something that you take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:46.320 --> 01:03:48.800
you know, a lot more seriously today&nbsp;
than you would have done a few years ago?&nbsp;

01:03:50.160 --> 01:03:54.720
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Definitely, David.&nbsp; I mean&nbsp;
for a number of reasons, not least because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:55.440 --> 01:04:00.480
obviously in my day job, we invest money&nbsp;
on behalf of pension funds and insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:00.480 --> 01:04:05.440
companies and endowments across the world.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
they have been some of the leading players in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:06.640 --> 01:04:12.080
pushing companies to sort of adopt targets&nbsp;
that make sense, pushing governments to adopt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:12.080 --> 01:04:16.720
targets that make sense.&nbsp; And so we’ve&nbsp;
responded ourselves by saying, well, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:20.640
we have ourselves to be really clear about&nbsp;
the journey we’re on in terms of ESG.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.200 --> 01:04:26.640
And I think that’s probably the biggest&nbsp;
point I’d make, which is it’s a journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:34.720
not a destination.&nbsp; So, whilst, you&nbsp;
know, we’re aiming as a world for 2% less&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:36.160 --> 01:04:40.560
in terms of the degree – the heated world,&nbsp;
actually that in itself will not be the end;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:40.560 --> 01:04:46.480
we still need to continue.&nbsp; And the innovation&nbsp;
we’re seeing from some companies across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:46.480 --> 01:04:49.840
spectrum, to deal with the issues that&nbsp;
have come about, are quite incredible.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:50.640 --> 01:04:55.040
And I think that’s probably one of the things that&nbsp;
excites me most.&nbsp; It’s that you can both address&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:55.040 --> 01:05:00.800
the issue and still get innovation and still get&nbsp;
growth without necessarily damaging the planet.&nbsp;

01:05:03.520 --> 01:05:07.360
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Wol.&nbsp; Well, Nick, I’ve&nbsp;
really enjoyed working with you for the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:07.360 --> 01:05:12.960
year.&nbsp; You’ve really moved the whole thing forward&nbsp;
a huge way, you’ve got complete support from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:13.520 --> 01:05:18.800
everyone in the NHS.&nbsp; And I think actually having&nbsp;
the NHS sort of brand, if you like, associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:18.800 --> 01:05:22.640
with this is changing the behaviour of many of&nbsp;
our – well, certainly many of our suppliers.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:23.360 --> 01:05:29.520
I mean, it’s quite interesting as well, isn’t it,&nbsp;
that the people who are really ahead of this are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:34.160
some of the big American tech companies?&nbsp; I mean,&nbsp;
I’m particularly – you know, if you take companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.160 --> 01:05:40.800
like Apple and Microsoft, this is absolutely&nbsp;
– I mean Microsoft are going to recover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:40.800 --> 01:05:45.280
not just stop, not just get to net zero, but&nbsp;
are going to recover all the carbon they’ve ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.280 --> 01:05:50.640
emitted since they became a company, what, 15&nbsp;
years ago or something.&nbsp; I mean, it is becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:51.440 --> 01:05:57.440
mainstream for big corporations.&nbsp;&nbsp;
So, Nick, have you got anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:57.440 --> 01:06:01.600
you want to just finish up with, or…?
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: No.&nbsp; All I was going to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:01.600 --> 01:06:07.120
was I agree with you, obviously.&nbsp; What is really&nbsp;
exciting is it extends beyond just those big&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.120 --> 01:06:13.440
corporations, right?&nbsp; You see small, medium-sized&nbsp;
enterprises across the country, urban centres in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:13.440 --> 01:06:21.040
England, rural populations across Australia&nbsp;
identifying that the future is a sustainable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.040 --> 01:06:25.360
future, and that’s where we’re all heading&nbsp;
towards, right?&nbsp; There’s a direction of travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:25.360 --> 01:06:29.680
and that is set and fixed.&nbsp; No one’s questioning&nbsp;
that anymore.&nbsp; The question is, are you ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:29.680 --> 01:06:35.120
to respond to that?&nbsp; Are you ready to lead that&nbsp;
journey?&nbsp; I’m really proud to be a part of the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:35.680 --> 01:06:40.160
as we do that for healthcare.&nbsp; I’m really excited&nbsp;
about where we’re going to end up with this.&nbsp;

01:06:40.160 --> 01:06:45.360
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good.&nbsp; So, Nick, next time we see&nbsp;
you, you’ll be driving that electric and hydrogen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:45.360 --> 01:06:50.880
ambulance up in Glasgow, I guess.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: That sounds dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:50.880 --> 01:06:54.640
but I might leave that to you, if that’s okay.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay.&nbsp; Well, thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:54.640 --> 01:06:56.320
indeed.&nbsp; Thank you.&nbsp;
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:56.320 --> 01:07:02.480
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right.&nbsp; Well, we’re moving on then&nbsp;
to the next item, which is Freedom to Speak Up.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:02.480 --> 01:07:11.440
I’m not sure whether Ann Radmore has joined us&nbsp;
quite yet.&nbsp; So, Andrew Morris, can I invite you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:11.440 --> 01:07:16.160
to introduce our two colleagues, our two Freedom&nbsp;
to Speak Up colleagues who are on the call&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:16.160 --> 01:07:22.320
and sort of hold the fort until Ann gets here?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: I think that, David, actually I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.320 --> 01:07:26.800
was going to introduce the item&nbsp;
after Andrew until Ann gets here.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:26.800 --> 01:07:31.120
But Andrew, why don’t you go first?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: No, you go ahead then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:33.040
Steve.&nbsp; Go on.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:33.040 --> 01:07:38.320
Well, I think we were going to – I was going&nbsp;
to say a little bit on my two years’ worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:38.320 --> 01:07:43.760
being Executive Lead for the internal Freedom to&nbsp;
Speak Up function and then bring in Nick and Gary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.760 --> 01:07:47.680
two of my guardians who are here today to give&nbsp;
their reflections and then hand over to Ann.&nbsp; But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:47.680 --> 01:07:52.800
Andrew, I’m sure it would be an opportunity for&nbsp;
you to come in as the Board sponsor as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:53.440 --> 01:08:00.560
So, as I’ve just said, I took over the role&nbsp;
of Executive Lead for Freedom to Speak Up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.560 --> 01:08:08.160
just over two years ago, and it’s been a real&nbsp;
privilege to do that.&nbsp; I do hope that we – well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:08.160 --> 01:08:12.640
I know that we have made some progress during&nbsp;
the two years.&nbsp; So, some of that is in the paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:12.640 --> 01:08:19.040
but I’d reflect on bringing the Freedom to Speak&nbsp;
Up guardians and processes from NHS England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.040 --> 01:08:24.080
NHS Improvement together into a much&nbsp;
more harmonised set of guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.080 --> 01:08:28.960
and harmonised set of processes, which we’ve&nbsp;
also strengthened to support the guardians.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.680 --> 01:08:35.840
We’ve increased the number of guardians&nbsp;
that we have to ensure that they are spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:36.880 --> 01:08:43.440
widely across our organisation, which of course&nbsp;
is both geographically spread and spread in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:43.440 --> 01:08:48.937
of individual directorates.&nbsp; We’ve also ensured&nbsp;
that the guardians reflect the diversity that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.937 --> 01:08:54.000
we have in – or reflects the diversity that&nbsp;
we have in all the staff at NHS England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:59.760
So, I think we’ve made some real progress, but&nbsp;
there is undoubtedly much more work to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:00.560 --> 01:09:07.040
And Ann will talk a little bit about next steps&nbsp;
when she comes on.&nbsp; But just a few final thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.040 --> 01:09:11.520
from me.&nbsp; So, first, I’d like to say we were&nbsp;
really fortunate.&nbsp; I’ve met with the guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:11.520 --> 01:09:15.200
regularly over the last few years, but we are&nbsp;
really fortunate that we have such passionate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:15.200 --> 01:09:21.520
committed Freedom to Speak Up guardians, who are&nbsp;
there to support our staff, and also to challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:21.520 --> 01:09:27.280
us back as senior leaders.&nbsp; That’s an extremely&nbsp;
important function of the guardian role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.640 --> 01:09:34.320
It’s vital that we support managers to listen&nbsp;
up, as well as empowering staff to speak up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.320 --> 01:09:38.560
&nbsp;So this is not just about speaking up; it’s&nbsp;
about listening as well, and we all have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:38.560 --> 01:09:43.680
responsibility there.&nbsp; And I know executive&nbsp;
directors are meeting with the guardians or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:48.720
supporting their guardians, but I think it is&nbsp;
really important that the executive team, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:55.520
senior leadership team, continue to do that within&nbsp;
their directorates.&nbsp; All directorates are – have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:55.520 --> 01:10:00.960
their own guardians and so meeting the guardians&nbsp;
on a regular basis, as well as empowering staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:00.960 --> 01:10:06.480
to speak up, I think is really important.&nbsp;
And the last reflection I wanted to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:06.480 --> 01:10:12.800
was to pay tribute to Henrietta Hughes, who has&nbsp;
stepped down recently after a five-year tenure as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:12.800 --> 01:10:17.920
National Guardian.&nbsp; Henrietta attended this&nbsp;
Board on a number of occasions when we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:17.920 --> 01:10:23.280
talked about Freedom to Speak Up, and she has&nbsp;
been a real support to me personally, and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:23.280 --> 01:10:30.320
to our organisation, as we have progressed this&nbsp;
agenda.&nbsp; She has helped us where we need help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:35.360
she’s challenged us appropriately where we&nbsp;
need challenging.&nbsp; And she’s really said to us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:35.360 --> 01:10:41.120
‘You need to be as good as the best trusts&nbsp;
out there, the best organisations in the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.760 --> 01:10:46.320
at Freedom to Speak Up.&nbsp; And she’s helped us&nbsp;
with some of the particular unique challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.320 --> 01:10:51.920
that we have around the geographical&nbsp;
dispersion of our staff, for instance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:52.720 --> 01:10:56.160
So, those are my reflections.&nbsp; But I think now&nbsp;
I want to bring in Nick and Gary – I don’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:56.160 --> 01:11:01.200
who’s going first but I’m sure you have decided&nbsp;
between you – two of our guardians, to give their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:07.440
reflections on the report and on the last year.
&gt;&gt;Nicholas Hodgetts: Thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:07.440 --> 01:11:13.600
Stephen.&nbsp; I will go first.&nbsp; My name is Nick&nbsp;
Hodgetts and I’m one of the Freedom to Speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:13.600 --> 01:11:19.840
Up guardians as Stephen has said.&nbsp; Gary and myself&nbsp;
have both been Freedom to Speak Up guardians for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.840 --> 01:11:25.200
some time now, to the point I think we’re regarded&nbsp;
as kind of senior guardians within the team.&nbsp; So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:25.200 --> 01:11:28.880
that’s probably part of the reason why we’re&nbsp;
here.&nbsp; We also have each other’s notes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.880 --> 01:11:32.720
so that if either of our technologies fall over,&nbsp;
the other one can dive in quite quickly.&nbsp; So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.680 --> 01:11:37.840
hopefully, we’re as prepared as we can be.&nbsp;
But thanks ever so much for the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.840 --> 01:11:45.760
enable us to speak at this meeting.&nbsp; As Stephen&nbsp;
has said, it has been a thrilling year in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:45.760 --> 01:11:50.240
of the progress of the Freedom to Speak Up&nbsp;
movement and everything that we have done.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:51.600 --> 01:11:55.280
Just as some reflections of, you&nbsp;
know, from our point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.920 --> 01:12:01.840
it’s been really good that things have risen so&nbsp;
significantly in the last year.&nbsp; I think Gary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:01.840 --> 01:12:06.880
will probably attest as well that in the first 12&nbsp;
months, it was very kind of – it was quite early&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.880 --> 01:12:13.440
days.&nbsp; I think although the Freedom to Speak Up&nbsp;
guardians came in first in about 2015/16 time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:14.000 --> 01:12:19.360
the system was still more or less in its infancy&nbsp;
in terms of what it was starting to deliver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:19.360 --> 01:12:24.960
But in the last 18/12 months in particular, the&nbsp;
amount of support for Freedom to Speak Up, both in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.960 --> 01:12:31.680
the organisation and in terms of the success it is&nbsp;
achieving, has risen significantly.&nbsp; I don’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:31.680 --> 01:12:36.560
to kind of re-emphasise things that are already in&nbsp;
the report, but it’s great that we’ve been able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:36.560 --> 01:12:43.280
increase, for example, from 17 Freedom to Speak&nbsp;
Up guardians up to 42 in the last 12 months.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:44.080 --> 01:12:49.360
as national Freedom to Speak Up Month comes around&nbsp;
again in October, we’ll be recruiting more numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.360 --> 01:12:54.720
to that list.&nbsp; And already, we’re at the point&nbsp;
where we need more people than we currently have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:55.440 --> 01:13:00.400
But it’s also enabled us – as well as resolving&nbsp;
some of our caseload, it’s also allowed us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:00.400 --> 01:13:06.000
do a lot more proactive work, promoting Freedom&nbsp;
to Speak Up across the organisation, across our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:11.040
directorates and our regions; and allows each&nbsp;
of the guardians to actually have an active role&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.680 --> 01:13:17.360
not only in caseload work, but in speaking&nbsp;
to our senior managers and our senior&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.360 --> 01:13:23.520
leaders and improving the profile of what we do.&nbsp;
We’ve also gained a grip on the reporting process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:23.520 --> 01:13:28.480
which is never as kind of sexy as hearing about&nbsp;
the impact.&nbsp; But again, it’s helped us to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:28.480 --> 01:13:36.560
real grip.&nbsp; We are now able to not only log our&nbsp;
casework more effectively; we are also logging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.560 --> 01:13:42.080
our promotion work and just seeing the quantity&nbsp;
of it as we kind of input it onto the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.080 --> 01:13:46.960
We are clearly doing a lot and getting out&nbsp;
there a lot.&nbsp; And more than that, people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:47.600 --> 01:13:51.840
bringing or plucking up the courage to speak&nbsp;
up in more numbers than they were previously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:52.560 --> 01:13:57.920
I think as well as that, there has been an&nbsp;
increase in the number of people who’ve reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:57.920 --> 01:14:03.520
to us on an anonymous basis, which kind of sits&nbsp;
one or two ways depending on how you look at it.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.520 --> 01:14:09.520
I think it’s partly a positive reflection because&nbsp;
more people are plucking up the courage to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:09.520 --> 01:14:14.720
up.&nbsp; It might be that at this stage they only feel&nbsp;
they’re able to kind of speak up, but may not feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:14.720 --> 01:14:19.360
safe identifying themselves.&nbsp; But I think the fact&nbsp;
that more people are speaking up is a good thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:20.080 --> 01:14:24.720
It does, of course, present the challenge that it&nbsp;
would be great in the ideal world if people feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.720 --> 01:14:31.280
safe enough to speak up and know that they can do&nbsp;
that, whilst identifying themselves and knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.280 --> 01:14:36.400
that we can take those things towards a successful&nbsp;
conclusion without kind of the fear of negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:36.400 --> 01:14:41.920
repercussions.&nbsp; That would be the ideal, but it’s&nbsp;
a challenge that we are continuing to work on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.920 --> 01:14:45.840
And I think as well – and this was a&nbsp;
very significant meeting that we had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:46.800 --> 01:14:50.160
one of the meetings we had last week with our&nbsp;
non-executive directors, some of whom are on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:50.160 --> 01:14:56.880
this call – we do have a lot more regular&nbsp;
support and interaction both as a team&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:56.880 --> 01:15:01.200
and also with the executive leadership and the&nbsp;
non-executive directors for Freedom to Speak Up.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.000 --> 01:15:05.680
And even they were saying to us last week&nbsp;
that even though some of the conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:05.680 --> 01:15:10.400
we have are difficult, and even though some&nbsp;
of the cases we end up getting involved in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:10.400 --> 01:15:16.080
which might involve kind of senior levels, might&nbsp;
meet with difficult conversations, on occasions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:16.080 --> 01:15:22.080
resistance, the fact that the conversations&nbsp;
are happening in the first place is seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:22.080 --> 01:15:28.240
a major step forward and is seen as a positive&nbsp;
that culture change is starting to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:28.240 --> 01:15:33.280
It might be happening and might be happening at&nbsp;
a fairly slow pace, it might take a long time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:33.280 --> 01:15:39.520
fully kind of transform things into the way that&nbsp;
people would ideally like them to be.&nbsp; But getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:39.520 --> 01:15:44.080
that endorsement from the people at a senior&nbsp;
level, for which we’re hugely grateful; getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:44.080 --> 01:15:49.120
that sense that a real difference is being made&nbsp;
not just in terms of resolving people’s individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:49.120 --> 01:15:54.960
cases is, I think, a massive endorsement to&nbsp;
Freedom to Speak Up and a massive step forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:54.960 --> 01:16:01.200
And the final thing I will say, before I hand over&nbsp;
to Gary, is that another major issue that’s come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.200 --> 01:16:05.520
up, a positive issue that’s come up in the&nbsp;
last few months, there’s a brief mention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:05.520 --> 01:16:09.200
it in the report.&nbsp; But I wanted to kind of&nbsp;
mention it because I think it’s important:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:09.920 --> 01:16:14.480
the links of Freedom to Speak Up into the wider&nbsp;
work that supports the overall People Plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:15.040 --> 01:16:20.640
We are starting to regularly get approached&nbsp;
to become involved in bullying harassment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:21.760 --> 01:16:30.160
involved in directorate OD work, into EDI, into&nbsp;
civility.&nbsp; Freedom to Speak Up isn’t just making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:35.520
a difference in its own silo, if you like.&nbsp; It’s&nbsp;
hugely successful there, but it’s also becoming a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:35.520 --> 01:16:41.280
key part of the overall People Plan and supporting&nbsp;
seven ways of working across the organisation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:41.280 --> 01:16:45.440
And that I think is a massive thing, because&nbsp;
it means Freedom to Speak Up isn’t just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:46.000 --> 01:16:50.800
Freedom to Speak Up with those capital&nbsp;
letters; it’s freedom to speak up as a concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:50.800 --> 01:16:54.960
a wider concept, linking that into&nbsp;
the strategic work that we’re doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:54.960 --> 01:16:59.120
There will be people who are speaking up,&nbsp;
who may not come via the formal process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:00.080 --> 01:17:04.720
but just come to us informally; or start&nbsp;
to speak to colleagues elsewhere in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:04.720 --> 01:17:09.520
organisation that may not be guardians.&nbsp;&nbsp;
But it’s getting – it’s starting to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:09.520 --> 01:17:14.000
it’s getting out there.&nbsp; The increased number&nbsp;
of formal cases is going up, but the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.000 --> 01:17:18.080
additional work and the strategic links on top of&nbsp;
that, I think, are even bigger success stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:18.800 --> 01:17:23.520
So, we’re hugely grateful for all of your support&nbsp;
and hopefully can continue to count on it.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:24.160 --> 01:17:25.840
And I will hand over to my colleague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:25.840 --> 01:17:30.320
Gary.&nbsp; Thank you very much.
&gt;&gt;Gary Slegg: Thanks, Nick, and hello, everyone.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:30.320 --> 01:17:35.200
I just wanted to quickly just give out three top&nbsp;
priorities for improving speaking-up culture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:35.920 --> 01:17:40.320
As Steve mentioned, we think it’s very important&nbsp;
that there’s visible support from the executive.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:40.880 --> 01:17:45.040
So to normalise speaking up, we think it is&nbsp;
essential we continue to receive the visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.040 --> 01:17:49.280
support from our executive directors&nbsp;
to bring about this cultural change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.280 --> 01:17:54.240
whereby so all grades can feel confident that&nbsp;
they won’t suffer detriments if they do speak up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.880 --> 01:17:58.240
So, executive directors have committed&nbsp;
to participate in Speak Up Month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.240 --> 01:18:03.200
which is in October, by hosting webinars for&nbsp;
their teams and making individual pledges to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:03.200 --> 01:18:08.400
address perceived barriers.&nbsp; But we would like&nbsp;
this to continue once Speak Up Month is over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:09.920 --> 01:18:15.120
Secondly, there’s the issue of leadership&nbsp;
development.&nbsp; So, the November 2020 staff survey&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:15.120 --> 01:18:20.800
indicated that the continued people interventions&nbsp;
and leadership efforts had had a positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:20.800 --> 01:18:26.800
impact.&nbsp; However, there’s still room for&nbsp;
improvement, particularly regarding equality and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.800 --> 01:18:32.720
diversity and inclusion, bullying and harassment,&nbsp;
immediate managers, and health and wellbeing.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:33.760 --> 01:18:38.320
Some staff report that they’ve lacked their&nbsp;
objectives and report feeling undervalued&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.320 --> 01:18:43.920
by their line managers.&nbsp; Individuals can&nbsp;
be unused to giving or receiving feedback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:43.920 --> 01:18:49.280
and success at work is often not celebrated.&nbsp;&nbsp;
In addition, some perceive that the organisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.280 --> 01:18:54.320
is not always equitable in its approach&nbsp;
to equality, diversity and inclusion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.320 --> 01:18:58.640
So, we think leadership development should&nbsp;
focus upon training all staff to work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:58.640 --> 01:19:04.640
to set clear goals and priorities, to receive&nbsp;
feedback and listen to those who are speaking up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:05.680 --> 01:19:08.800
And finally, there’s the issue of&nbsp;
tackling the barriers to speaking up.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:09.760 --> 01:19:14.320
The advocacy and learning team recently&nbsp;
completed a research projects into barriers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.320 --> 01:19:18.640
that prevent speaking up.&nbsp; We’d like to&nbsp;
see the lessons learnt from this research&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:18.640 --> 01:19:22.480
implemented, to encourage more people&nbsp;
to speak up when they have concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:23.120 --> 01:19:27.360
Barriers identified included a previous&nbsp;
poor experience of speaking up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:31.920
proving it’s not worthwhile, and a perception&nbsp;
that senior leadership really just aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:31.920 --> 01:19:36.960
interested and the fear of repercussions.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
these barriers need to be removed to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.960 --> 01:19:42.880
a true speak-up culture.&nbsp; Thanks very much.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Gary.&nbsp; I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:42.880 --> 01:19:48.480
that Ann, you’ve joined us.&nbsp; In case people don’t,&nbsp;
or everyone doesn’t know her, Ann is our Regional&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:49.040 --> 01:19:54.320
Director for the East of England and has been with&nbsp;
the NHS for a long, long time, haven’t you, Ann?&nbsp;

01:19:54.320 --> 01:19:56.640
&gt;&gt;Ann Radmore: Thank you for that, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Would you like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:59.280 --> 01:20:03.200
to – I mean, you’ve sort of come in at the end&nbsp;
of things. You were on at the beginning.&nbsp; That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.200 --> 01:20:07.520
unfortunate.&nbsp; We’re running a little ahead&nbsp;
of time, but would you like to give us your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:07.520 --> 01:20:12.480
reflections on this, on the speaking-up programme?
&gt;&gt;Ann Radmore: Yes, thanks very much, David.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:12.480 --> 01:20:17.280
I did manage to catch most of Nick and Gary’s&nbsp;
contribution, and I’m sure Steve was giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.280 --> 01:20:23.440
his reflections on a busy couple of years.&nbsp;
So, I hope you feel that the report in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:23.440 --> 01:20:29.920
of the Board today reflects that there’s been&nbsp;
real progress made.&nbsp; And I think to Nick’s point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:29.920 --> 01:20:35.600
Freedom to Speak Up is stepping into that space,&nbsp;
where more broadly it’s about creating an open&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:35.600 --> 01:20:41.200
culture, but there still remains a lot to do.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
I suspect it might be one of those topics where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.200 --> 01:20:46.000
there will always be more to do actually, and the&nbsp;
Board might want to think about that further.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.000 --> 01:20:52.240
So the direction the Board set some time ago has&nbsp;
clearly been followed, and we can see a tangible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:52.240 --> 01:20:57.840
network of people and processes that now exist&nbsp;
and are delivering the benefits my colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.840 --> 01:21:04.400
were describing.&nbsp; The numbers reporting do remain&nbsp;
low, although they have increased.&nbsp; And further&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:04.400 --> 01:21:09.120
reporting and speaking up, and particularly&nbsp;
as Nick was saying, moving to being confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:09.120 --> 01:21:13.840
to do that with the name attached, I think is a&nbsp;
thing we have to aspire to over the next year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.120 --> 01:21:20.000
And the broader work that was begun in 2020&nbsp;
during the height of the pandemic on our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.000 --> 01:21:25.040
seven behaviours, and the culture work that&nbsp;
Prerana has been leading, has created that context&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:25.040 --> 01:21:30.800
and the drive for the work.&nbsp; And there will&nbsp;
continue to be real resonance and benefit between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:30.800 --> 01:21:36.880
both.&nbsp; There are a number of recommendations in&nbsp;
the paper that you have seen, and I’ll be working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.880 --> 01:21:41.920
with our guardians to build those into a programme&nbsp;
of work for the next year, so we’re clear about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:41.920 --> 01:21:48.400
how the things we aspire to do can be delivered.&nbsp;
And you’ve just heard from Greg – from Gary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.400 --> 01:21:53.360
sorry, the priorities that come directly&nbsp;
from our guardians where we should put&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.360 --> 01:21:59.040
our time.&nbsp; And I think they resonate strongly&nbsp;
with my personal reflection coming into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.040 --> 01:22:06.160
SRO role here.&nbsp; For me, the two most impactful&nbsp;
changes are going to be shifting the culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.160 --> 01:22:11.920
and increasing that senior leadership.&nbsp; And&nbsp;
we are absolutely seeing that.&nbsp; Gary mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:12.480 --> 01:22:17.920
Freedom to Speak Up Month, and my NHS executive&nbsp;
colleagues taking an active lead in that period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.720 --> 01:22:23.680
but also the discussions that my executive&nbsp;
director colleagues have now committed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.680 --> 01:22:29.680
with their own guardians in their directorates, on&nbsp;
a quarterly basis, reflecting both on individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:29.680 --> 01:22:36.800
cases, but also on process and openness; and also,&nbsp;
the use of data that we’re now sharing regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.800 --> 01:22:43.040
with executive leads, so that they can see the&nbsp;
level of activity within their own directorate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:43.040 --> 01:22:47.440
I think the second area for me is absolutely&nbsp;
what Gary was recommending to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.440 --> 01:22:53.760
which is the ability to give and receive and hear&nbsp;
feedback throughout the organisation.&nbsp; And that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:53.760 --> 01:22:59.040
again, is a piece of work that Prerana has&nbsp;
led and developing that capability.&nbsp; And it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:59.040 --> 01:23:04.560
something we clearly need to work at further, but&nbsp;
I think we’ve started to step into that space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:05.440 --> 01:23:11.200
So, I’s welcome any reflections, David, that the&nbsp;
Board might have, as we shape next year’s work.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:11.200 --> 01:23:15.280
We need to continue, I believe, to&nbsp;
be guided by what we hear from staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:16.000 --> 01:23:21.200
what we hear from our increasingly skilled&nbsp;
group of guardians and the development&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:21.200 --> 01:23:27.440
approach that Prerana and the HR and OD team are&nbsp;
developing; and the emerging evidence, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:27.440 --> 01:23:34.240
of how all that is coming together to increase&nbsp;
the quality and culture of our own organisation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:34.240 --> 01:23:38.240
And as part of that embedding, I’m&nbsp;
delighted to say that so Andrew as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:38.880 --> 01:23:44.400
the non-executive lead and I will be meeting with&nbsp;
our new chief exec to review the Freedom to Speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:44.400 --> 01:23:49.520
Up work programme at the beginning of November.&nbsp;&nbsp;
And I think that gives us a real anchor point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:49.520 --> 01:23:53.400
to set that programme for the year to come.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ann, thank you very much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:53.400 --> 01:23:57.840
that and for your leadership in this area.&nbsp; It’s&nbsp;
been invaluable.&nbsp; So, we’re going to come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.840 --> 01:24:02.880
Wol and Laura, and then let Andrew and maybe&nbsp;
Prerana have a last word on this.&nbsp; So, Wol.&nbsp;

01:24:02.880 --> 01:24:09.520
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thanks, David.&nbsp; I do agree&nbsp;
with your comments around Henrietta and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.520 --> 01:24:12.880
leadership, you know, we were – was very&nbsp;
valuable.&nbsp; And I still see her, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:12.880 --> 01:24:16.560
because we’re together on another board, which is&nbsp;
great, and she’s being equally challenging there.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:17.280 --> 01:24:22.400
And that takes me to the question.&nbsp;
So one of the things which I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.400 --> 01:24:30.080
we invited Henrietta to do was to look to the&nbsp;
next mountain and to challenge us as a Board to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.080 --> 01:24:35.760
look to that.&nbsp; And so I’d ask the question, what&nbsp;
else can we be doing?&nbsp; What else is this stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:35.760 --> 01:24:39.360
target above and beyond all this?&nbsp;
And the second question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:39.920 --> 01:24:42.800
on this journey towards improving&nbsp;
culture, which is what this is all about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:43.360 --> 01:24:49.440
kind of where are we on a scale of one to ten?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ann, why not pick those two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:49.440 --> 01:24:53.440
questions up now?&nbsp; Prerana might want to&nbsp;
try and answer the second question at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:53.440 --> 01:24:58.880
end as well, but Ann, over to you first.
&gt;&gt;Ann Radmore: Thank you.&nbsp; I mean, I have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:58.880 --> 01:25:04.160
personal view, but I think probably the second&nbsp;
one is Prerana’s to respond more generally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:06.720 --> 01:25:12.960
I think the stretch target would be to move to&nbsp;
some kind of adult-to-adult relationship where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:12.960 --> 01:25:18.480
we can discuss difficult issues.&nbsp; And I think I&nbsp;
could take you to places in our many thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:18.480 --> 01:25:23.200
of staff where that’s happening, and we’ve got&nbsp;
that in reality.&nbsp; I could equally take you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:23.200 --> 01:25:30.160
places where it clearly isn’t.&nbsp; And so I think if&nbsp;
we go outside our current construct, outside our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.160 --> 01:25:35.680
organisation, what you read about and hear about&nbsp;
is it’s that adult-to-adult relationship that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:35.680 --> 01:25:41.280
fundamentally makes a difference.&nbsp; And if I think&nbsp;
to my time when I was leading NHS organisations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:42.240 --> 01:25:46.800
where they really hummed and worked for the&nbsp;
benefit of patients and were a great place&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:46.800 --> 01:25:51.520
to work, we’d got that right balance.&nbsp; So&nbsp;
that for me would be the stretch target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:51.520 --> 01:25:53.360
I’m sure your follow-on question will be, ‘Ann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:53.360 --> 01:25:57.280
how do we get there?’&nbsp; I might need a bit&nbsp;
longer to reflect on that, Wol, if that’s okay.&nbsp;

01:25:58.880 --> 01:26:02.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ann.&nbsp; Laura?
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: Yes, before Prerana kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:02.960 --> 01:26:08.720
of probably picks up both of the questions,&nbsp;
Henrietta’s impact looms large because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:08.720 --> 01:26:13.600
was actually going to sort of reflect on&nbsp;
something that I feel I heard from her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:13.600 --> 01:26:23.920
which was almost thinking of this as a three-part&nbsp;
journey; the sort of ‘speak up, listen up and act&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:23.920 --> 01:26:29.920
up’.&nbsp; I can’t remember if I’ve got that last one&nbsp;
right, but the sense of it feels like we’ve made&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:30.880 --> 01:26:36.080
a lot of progress with the speak up.&nbsp; I think&nbsp;
quite a lot of what I think I’m hearing from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:36.080 --> 01:26:42.480
the guardians is actually how do we embed more&nbsp;
in our leadership approach around listening up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:42.480 --> 01:26:46.000
and then probably the same through&nbsp;
to act up.&nbsp; So I guess that would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:46.000 --> 01:26:53.520
my challenges, that how do we increasingly frame&nbsp;
this in those three ways because I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:53.520 --> 01:26:59.600
very much shows what everyone’s part is?
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:59.600 --> 01:27:03.200
Ann, do you want to respond to that?
&gt;&gt;Ann Radmore: It’s certainly a construct&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:03.200 --> 01:27:09.840
that I have explored as I’ve come into this role,&nbsp;
Laura, I would absolutely agree.&nbsp; And I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:09.840 --> 01:27:14.320
if taken together, they are an articulation&nbsp;
of that adult-to-adult relationship I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:15.040 --> 01:27:21.440
just talking about.&nbsp; I am going to defer to&nbsp;
Prerana because I’m very clear that I want to nest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:21.440 --> 01:27:26.400
this piece of work within our broader cultural&nbsp;
piece.&nbsp; So, I think I’ll defer to Prerana, David.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:26.400 --> 01:27:34.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: All right, thank you, Ann.&nbsp; Andrew.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: So, I think if you work on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:34.000 --> 01:27:40.800
principle that everyone has a voice and a right&nbsp;
to be heard, we’ve moved mountains.&nbsp; We’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.800 --> 01:27:49.360
really made some good progress.&nbsp; I think we are&nbsp;
listening, but we don’t always act, to pick up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:49.360 --> 01:27:58.080
Laura’s point.&nbsp; And it’s the middle tier of the&nbsp;
organisation that’s probably the weakest link.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:58.080 --> 01:28:02.800
And I know Prerana is on the case,&nbsp;
but we need to support managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:02.800 --> 01:28:09.440
on how to engage and how to listen and act&nbsp;
and do this in a positive way.&nbsp; And it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:09.440 --> 01:28:16.240
part of our cultural change in terms of becoming&nbsp;
a more open kind of organisation going forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:16.240 --> 01:28:22.400
So, when I compare it with where we were three&nbsp;
years ago, boy, have we moved on.&nbsp; It’s, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:22.400 --> 01:28:29.600
really refreshing to see the progress and, as&nbsp;
importantly, the enthusiasm from our guardians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:29.600 --> 01:28:34.720
because they are fantastic.&nbsp; They are people&nbsp;
we should treasure.&nbsp; They’re really good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.720 --> 01:28:40.960
barometers of, you know, what the organisation&nbsp;
feels and thinks at any given point in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:41.520 --> 01:28:48.000
So, I think we’ve made some really good progress,&nbsp;
but we haven’t quite mastered the acting bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:49.280 --> 01:28:54.400
and I think there’s more work to be done.&nbsp;&nbsp;
But there’s exec team support for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.040 --> 01:29:01.520
so hopefully, you know, we will see it cascade&nbsp;
down through the structure over a period of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:01.520 --> 01:29:06.240
time.&nbsp; So, I’m really cheered by the progress&nbsp;
that we’ve made so far.&nbsp; It’s been quite a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.240 --> 01:29:13.440
fantastic journey to be part of, so thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Andrew.&nbsp; Prerana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:13.440 --> 01:29:17.040
last word from you.&nbsp; And maybe you could&nbsp;
pick up Wol’s second question about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:17.040 --> 01:29:19.360
where we are on the journey.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Absolutely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:19.360 --> 01:29:25.280
thank you so much.&nbsp; And it’s been great to work&nbsp;
very closely with Steve and also now with Ann&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:25.280 --> 01:29:30.320
on this very important element.&nbsp;
So, as Andrew and others have said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:30.320 --> 01:29:35.280
actually, ‘I have a voice’ is a key part of the&nbsp;
People Promise, which is part of the People Plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:36.080 --> 01:29:41.680
And I have a voice that counts in shaping my&nbsp;
work, I should have a voice that counts in shaping&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:41.680 --> 01:29:48.000
my contribution, in creating improvement, in&nbsp;
creating positive change.&nbsp; And what we are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:48.000 --> 01:29:53.200
is making sure that we are gathering evidence&nbsp;
about how the changes are impacting people’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.200 --> 01:29:59.840
you know, work life and their delivery.&nbsp;
So, we have for the last 15 months been doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:59.840 --> 01:30:05.440
monthly check-in surveys.&nbsp; As soon as the pandemic&nbsp;
started, we started a monthly staff survey, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:05.440 --> 01:30:11.600
tells us in real time where people are and where&nbsp;
managers need to focus and teams need to focus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:12.560 --> 01:30:16.880
Our annual staff survey, of course, showed us&nbsp;
actually that we’ve made progress in a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:16.880 --> 01:30:22.480
of areas, including listening and two-way&nbsp;
communication between managers, leaders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.040 --> 01:30:29.680
their teams; and also identified some areas of&nbsp;
focus, including line manager skills.&nbsp; So, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:29.680 --> 01:30:34.800
have invested in training for managers, especially&nbsp;
virtual management: how do you lead virtually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:34.800 --> 01:30:40.560
how do you manage a team virtually; leadership&nbsp;
development; and also, looking at how we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:40.560 --> 01:30:45.040
much more of an improvement mindset, as we&nbsp;
– you know, as across the health service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:45.040 --> 01:30:50.240
that’s something that we want to enhance.&nbsp;
And the closer we can get to the service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:50.240 --> 01:30:53.840
the closer we can get to patients,&nbsp;
the more open the culture will be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:53.840 --> 01:31:00.240
because ultimately if someone is giving ideas&nbsp;
and suggestions about how we are going to enhance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:00.240 --> 01:31:05.200
our delivery and our service, I mean, we&nbsp;
absolutely have to be welcoming that.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:05.200 --> 01:31:09.360
that’s why we all – you know, we’re all at work.
It doesn’t always happen like that, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:09.360 --> 01:31:14.960
Ann was saying, and as our Freedom to Speak Up&nbsp;
guardians have shared as well.&nbsp; So, we have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:14.960 --> 01:31:21.200
People Plan for NHSEI, and we measure progress&nbsp;
on it through our staff surveys, which are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:21.840 --> 01:31:26.480
we’re going to move to quarterly, so that we have&nbsp;
some time to make adjustments.&nbsp; And the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:26.480 --> 01:31:32.080
the NHS is moving to a quarterly survey as well.&nbsp;
We are able to get down to a team level&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:32.080 --> 01:31:37.360
when we look at those scores.&nbsp; And so again,&nbsp;
we can make much more targeted interventions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:37.360 --> 01:31:42.080
in terms of supporting managers and supporting&nbsp;
teams in order to do that.&nbsp; So, evidence-based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:43.120 --> 01:31:49.280
you know, taking the People Promise and focused on&nbsp;
our delivery for the service.&nbsp; Thank you, David.&nbsp;

01:31:49.280 --> 01:31:52.480
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Prerana, you haven’t –&nbsp;
you have deliberately not answered Wol’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:52.480 --> 01:31:54.240
question, I think.&nbsp;
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:54.240 --> 01:31:57.840
Which is how far are we on the journey?&nbsp;
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, how far along are we?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:59.040 --> 01:32:02.240
I think it’s a fair question.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: No, well, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:02.800 --> 01:32:06.480
it would have to be – which is why I went to&nbsp;
the staff survey, because I think what I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:06.480 --> 01:32:10.320
want to know is where does the majority of the&nbsp;
staff think we are on the journey.&nbsp; And they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:10.320 --> 01:32:17.040
saying that I get – you know, they’re much more&nbsp;
engaged with leaders and with managers.&nbsp; Overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.040 --> 01:32:22.800
engagement has gone up.&nbsp; Advocacy about ‘would I&nbsp;
recommend this as a place to work’, NHSEI went up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:22.800 --> 01:32:28.720
by double percentage points in the – during the&nbsp;
pandemic, actually.&nbsp; But in areas where they said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:28.720 --> 01:32:35.280
line managers need more support and need to have&nbsp;
stronger skills in order to, you know, in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:35.280 --> 01:32:39.840
create a better environment, there are areas that&nbsp;
we haven’t progressed as much as we would like.&nbsp;

01:32:39.840 --> 01:32:43.760
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And that, of course, applies,&nbsp;
you know, from the feedback we have on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:43.760 --> 01:32:47.360
workforce rates of quality standard, you know,&nbsp;
we’ve got a lot more work to do, haven’t we?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:48.560 --> 01:32:55.600
Anyway, thank you very much for that, Prerana, but&nbsp;
– and, Ann, thank you very much for joining us.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:55.600 --> 01:32:59.360
And Gary and Nick, thank you both, you know,&nbsp;
very much for taking the time out to be with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:59.360 --> 01:33:04.800
us today.&nbsp; The work you’re doing is incredibly&nbsp;
important as we try and change the whole culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:04.800 --> 01:33:09.360
within which we’re working in the NHS.&nbsp; So,&nbsp;
thank you, all three of you, very much indeed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:09.360 --> 01:33:11.200
&gt;&gt;Nicholas Hodgetts: Thanks&nbsp;
ever so much, everybody.&nbsp;

01:33:11.200 --> 01:33:17.280
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you.&nbsp; So, just two things,&nbsp;
just I think for noting.&nbsp; There’s item six, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:17.280 --> 01:33:21.040
mandate.&nbsp; Ian, you don’t want to talk about that,&nbsp;
presumably?&nbsp; We’re just noting that, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:21.040 --> 01:33:27.360
and the complaints policy we are noting&nbsp;
as well.&nbsp; Just before we get to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:29.280 --> 01:33:31.680
Ian, your hand is up, is it?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I just wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:31.680 --> 01:33:37.440
say very briefly that the mandate is obviously a&nbsp;
really important part of the formal accountability&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:37.440 --> 01:33:44.240
of NHS England as a statutory arm’s length&nbsp;
body of government to government.&nbsp; And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:44.240 --> 01:33:49.280
paper kind of briefly lifts the lid on the&nbsp;
government’s assessment of how we performed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:49.280 --> 01:33:57.040
during 2020 to 2021.&nbsp; And I think it both reflects&nbsp;
the intrinsic difficulty of some of the challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:57.040 --> 01:34:02.960
in relation to elective recovery, urgent&nbsp;
and emergency care and dental activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:03.600 --> 01:34:09.120
but also the huge achievements&nbsp;
by our own staff and NHS staff in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:09.120 --> 01:34:15.040
responding to the pandemic.&nbsp; Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian.&nbsp; If there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:15.040 --> 01:34:21.840
any – no other business, I’d just like to say&nbsp;
one thing.&nbsp; This is Dido’s last Board meeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.840 --> 01:34:27.520
Actually Dido was on my interview panel when&nbsp;
they made that terribly mistaken decision to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:28.320 --> 01:34:34.480
make me the Chairman of NHS England.&nbsp; But I’ve&nbsp;
just been noting, as we had the discussion today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:34.480 --> 01:34:41.200
that the People Plan must have been mentioned 25,&nbsp;
30 times.&nbsp; You know, it’s become an integral part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:41.200 --> 01:34:46.640
of the way that the culture is changing&nbsp;
within the NHS, Dido, and you were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:47.200 --> 01:34:53.440
principal architect along with Julian Hartley,&nbsp;
I think, at the time, of the People Plan.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:53.440 --> 01:34:59.920
you’ve made a huge contribution to the NHS.
If I had to pick one word, Dido,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:59.920 --> 01:35:04.160
that – and limited myself one word to&nbsp;
describe you, it would be courage.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:05.200 --> 01:35:09.440
I think the way that you – what you’ve been&nbsp;
through and the way you’ve picked yourself up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:10.080 --> 01:35:18.160
and come up smiling, bearing no grudges, is a huge&nbsp;
testament to you and a huge example to all of us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:18.160 --> 01:35:23.360
So, on behalf of everybody on the Board, I’m&nbsp;
sure, and through your many friends in the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:24.160 --> 01:35:27.440
Dido, a very, very big thank you.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: David, thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:27.440 --> 01:35:32.720
so very much for that, and it’s been&nbsp;
a privilege to work alongside you and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.720 --> 01:35:38.480
everyone on the Board and even more so a privilege&nbsp;
to be part of the NHS family.&nbsp; So, thank you all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:38.480 --> 01:35:45.360
very much, and I hope we all stay close. &nbsp;
I think the meeting today, although it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:45.360 --> 01:35:50.240
incredibly challenging times, it’s both heartening&nbsp;
and I think really encouraging to see the agenda&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:50.240 --> 01:35:56.640
that Amanda and the team is setting out.&nbsp; And I’m&nbsp;
sure we will all benefit from their endeavours&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:56.640 --> 01:36:16.320
going forward.&nbsp; So, thank you.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:16.320 --> 01:36:18.000
I think the meeting has been drawn to a close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:21.360 --> 01:36:29.280
silently.
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:29.280 --> 01:36:31.420
Thanks all.&nbsp; Bye.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Bye.&nbsp;

01:36:31.420 --> 01:36:33.605
